\title{\textbf{GIR: Gini Index Regression}\\
\large Estimation, inference and prediction: detailed reference for \code{gir()}, \code{bboot.gir()}, and \code{predict.gir()}}
\author{David D\'iaz-Villarejo}
\date{Package version 0.2.1 --- October 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle
\tableofcontents
\newpage

\section{Getting started}

GIR estimates how the conditional Gini index varies with covariates. Use
\code{gir()} to fit the model, \code{bboot.gir()} for bootstrap inference,
and \code{predict()} to evaluate the fitted Gini index at chosen covariate
values. The method is described in the working paper by Bhardwaj and
D\'iaz-Villarejo \cite{girpaper}.

This example fits a separate Gini index for each of two groups. The data
include ties, which the conditional mid-distribution handles directly.
The code and its output are regenerated when the package is built.

<<quick-fit>>=
library(GIR)
d <- data.frame(y = rep(c(1, 2, 2, 4, 5), 8),
                group = factor(rep(c("a", "b"), each = 20)))
fit <- gir(y ~ 0 + group, data = d, discrete = "group")
coef(fit)
@

With group indicators in both stages, each coefficient is the empirical
Gini index for that group, without a finite-sample correction. Bootstrap
inference refits both stages using the same weights in each draw.

<<quick-inference>>=
bb <- bboot.gir(fit, R = 499L, seed = 42L)
confint(bb)
predict(fit, data.frame(group = c("a", "b")),
        interval = "confidence", bootstrap = bb)
@

The prediction intervals concern the conditional Gini index at each profile.
They are not intervals for individual outcomes or simultaneous confidence
bands. For more examples, including continuous covariates, see
Section~\ref{sec:worked}. The remaining sections describe the arguments,
calculations and diagnostics in detail.

\section{Model and interface}

\code{gir()} estimates the point coefficients of the linear Gini Index
Regression (GIR) model. Its user interface follows the formula/data conventions
of familiar R regression functions: the second-stage model is specified with a
two-sided formula, an intercept is included by default, and the fitted object
supports standard methods such as \code{summary()}, \code{coef()},
\code{fitted()}, \code{residuals()}, \code{model.frame()}, and
\code{model.matrix()}.

The package also provides \code{bboot.gir()} for two-stage Bayesian-bootstrap
inference and \code{predict.gir()} for linear conditional Gini predictions and
pointwise uncertainty. Their detailed references begin in
Sections~\ref{sec:bootstrap} and~\ref{sec:prediction}. The GIR-specific
goodness-of-fit statistic is an optional output of \code{gir()}.

The first stage estimates the conditional mid-distribution function
\[
\midcdf(y\mid x)
=
P(Y<y\mid X=x)+\frac12P(Y=y\mid X=x)
=
E\!\left[\psi_y(Y)\mid X=x\right],
\]
where
\[
\psi_y(t)=\mathbf 1\{t<y\}+\frac12\mathbf 1\{t=y\}.
\]
The mid-distribution definition applies to continuous, discrete, and mixed
outcomes. The paper's inference theory covers continuous and finite-discrete
response regimes, not mixed response laws. For a continuous
response, ties occur with probability zero and \(\midcdf(y\mid x)\)
coincides with the ordinary conditional CDF.

\section{Usage}

\begin{lstlisting}
gir(
  formula,
  data,
  subset,
  weights,
  na.action = stats::na.fail,

  first_stage = NULL,
  continuous = NULL,
  discrete = NULL,
  midcdf = NULL,

  basis = c("legendre", "spline"),
  kappa = NULL,
  basis_constant = 1,
  sieve_size = NULL,
  sieve_size_type = c("auto", "dimension", "knots"),
  sieve_size_reference = c("cell", "full"),
  spline_order = 4L,
  basis_bounds = NULL,

  clip = FALSE,

  first_stage_singular = c("error", "ridge", "drop", "none"),
  first_stage_tol = 1e-12,
  ridge = 1e-10,
  minimum_cell_size = 8L,
  max_basis_columns = 1000L,

  singular.ok = TRUE,
  second_stage_tol = 1e-7,

  GIR = FALSE,

  model = TRUE,
  x = FALSE,
  y = FALSE,
  contrasts = NULL
)

midcdf(object, ...)
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Mathematical estimator}

Let \(X_i\) denote the second-stage regressor vector created by the model
formula, including a constant unless the formula removes it. Let \(w_i\geq0\)
be the optional observation weight, with \(w_i=1\) by default. Once the first
stage has produced \(\mhat_i=\mhat(Y_i\mid X_i^{(1)})\), where
\(X_i^{(1)}\) denotes the chosen first-stage conditioning variables,
\code{gir()} computes
\[
\widehat A
=
\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n w_iY_iX_iX_i',
\qquad
\widehat b
=
\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n w_iX_iY_i\{2\mhat_i-1\},
\]
and solves
\[
\thetahat=\widehat A^{-1}\widehat b
\]
when \(\widehat A\) is nonsingular. Under rank deficiency and
\code{singular.ok = TRUE}, an estimable subset is obtained by pivoted QR and
aliased coefficients are reported as \code{NA}.

For a discrete cell \(g\), define
\(\mathcal I_g=\{i:D_i=g\}\), and let \(p_{K_g}(Z_i)\) be the basis vector for
the continuous first-stage variables. The cell-specific first stage used for
observation \(i\in\mathcal I_g\) is
\[
\mhat_i
=
p_{K_g}(Z_i)'
\left\{
\sum_{j\in\mathcal I_g}w_jp_{K_g}(Z_j)p_{K_g}(Z_j)'
\right\}^{-1}
\left\{
\sum_{j\in\mathcal I_g}w_jp_{K_g}(Z_j)
\left[
\mathbf 1\{Y_j<Y_i\}+\frac12\mathbf 1\{Y_j=Y_i\}
\right]
\right\}.
\]
The implementation sorts \(Y\) once within each cell and obtains all
threshold-specific right-hand sides through cumulative sums. It therefore does
not run one separate least-squares regression for every observed threshold.

\section{Arguments}

\subsection*{\code{formula}}

A two-sided R model formula for the linear GIR second stage. The left-hand side
must be one numeric response. The right-hand side is processed by
\code{model.matrix()}, so ordinary formula syntax, factors, interactions, and
transformations are available. A constant is included by default. Use
\code{0 +} or \code{-1} to remove it.

For example,
\begin{lstlisting}
y ~ treatment + age + factor(region)
\end{lstlisting}
contains an intercept, while
\begin{lstlisting}
y ~ 0 + x1 + x2 + x3
\end{lstlisting}
does not. The latter convention corresponds to the paper's simulation designs,
whose second-stage regressors carry no constant.

Offsets are not implemented and generate an error.

\subsection*{\code{data}}

A data frame, or an object coercible to a data frame, containing all variables
used by the second stage, first stage, weights, subset expression, and any
supplied mid-CDF column.

\subsection*{\code{subset}}

An optional logical, numeric, or character row index evaluated in \code{data}.
The subset is applied before missing-value processing. Vectors supplied through
\code{weights} or \code{midcdf} may have length one, \code{nrow(data)}, or the
number of selected rows.

\subsection*{\code{weights}}

Optional nonnegative case or sampling weights. They enter both stages:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
\item in the cell-specific first-stage Gram matrices and threshold scores; and
\item in the second-stage moments \(\widehat A\) and \(\widehat b\).
\end{itemize}

At least one weight in the retained estimation sample must be strictly
positive, after subsetting and missing-value handling. Negative or infinite weights
produce an error; missing weights are handled by \code{na.action} together
with the other variables. These weights are empirical-moment weights; they should not be
interpreted automatically as inverse conditional-variance weights.

\subsection*{\code{na.action}}

The missing-data action. The default, \code{stats::na.fail}, stops if any
variable needed by either stage, the weights, or a supplied mid-CDF contains a
missing value. \code{na.omit} and \code{na.exclude} remove incomplete rows and
produce a warning reporting how many observations were removed. All objects are
then aligned to the same estimation sample. Missing weights are treated like
any other missing variable: they stop the fit under \code{na.fail} and their
rows are removed under \code{na.omit} or \code{na.exclude}.

An initially empty dataset, an empty subset, and an empty sample after
missing-value removal each have explicit errors. If all selected observations
are removed, the error names the action and selected sample size. If rows
remain but all their weights are zero, a separate retained-weight error is
issued. These checks precede matrix construction and the routine removal
warning, including when R warnings are promoted to errors with
\code{options(warn=2)}. A nonempty sample of zero outcomes is a different
problem: the second-stage moments have zero rank.

Custom actions must return a data frame selecting original rows with valid,
unique row identifiers. Their removal warning counts incomplete and complete
rows separately. An error inside a custom action is reported as a custom-action
failure, retaining its original message; it is not automatically attributed
to missing values.

\subsection*{\code{first_stage}}

Selects the variables conditioning the nonparametric first-stage estimate.
Accepted forms are:

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2.2em]
\item \code{NULL}: use the raw variables appearing on the right-hand side of
      \code{formula};
\item a one-sided formula, such as \code{~ x1 + x2 + region}; or
\item a character vector of data-column names.
\end{enumerate}

The selection is based on underlying data variables. For example,
\code{first_stage = ~ x + I(x^2)} identifies \code{x} as the raw continuous
variable; the nonlinear series transformations are generated internally.

The first-stage conditioning set may differ from the second-stage regressors,
but such a choice changes the conditional mid-distribution being estimated and
should be justified econometrically.

\subsection*{\code{continuous}}

A character vector naming the continuous first-stage variables. Every named
column must be numeric and must also be included in \code{first_stage}.
Continuous variables are rescaled to \([0,1]\) using \code{basis_bounds} before
the basis is evaluated.

Variables not classified explicitly are classified automatically. Numeric
variables are classified as continuous by default. Therefore, a numeric dummy
or a numeric variable taking values \(\{1,2,3,4\}\) must be listed explicitly in
\code{discrete} when it is intended to define cells. A message names the
numeric columns that were classified by default. An undeclared numeric column
with at most two distinct values in the retained estimation sample is an error,
because a two-point support
cannot carry a continuous basis; declare it in \code{discrete}, or in
\code{continuous} if that is really intended.
This check runs after missing-value handling; values appearing only in
discarded observations do not count towards the retained support.

\subsection*{\code{discrete}}

A character vector naming the discrete first-stage variables. Factors, ordered
factors, character variables, and logical variables are automatically treated
as discrete when they have not been classified explicitly. Numeric discrete
variables should be listed explicitly.

The function forms cells from the observed joint combinations of all discrete
variables. It estimates the series regression separately within each observed
cell. Empty theoretical combinations are not created.

\paragraph{Example: one four-valued discrete regressor.}
Suppose \(X\) is continuous and \(D\in\{1,2,3,4\}\). The call
\begin{lstlisting}
fit <- gir(
  y ~ x + factor(d),
  data = dat,
  first_stage = ~ x + d,
  continuous = "x",
  discrete = "d",
  basis = "spline"
)
\end{lstlisting}
creates the four samples
\[
\mathcal I_1=\{i:D_i=1\},\quad
\mathcal I_2=\{i:D_i=2\},\quad
\mathcal I_3=\{i:D_i=3\},\quad
\mathcal I_4=\{i:D_i=4\},
\]
and separately estimates
\(M(y\mid x,D=1),\ldots,M(y\mid x,D=4)\). The fitted values are then returned
to their original observation order before the common second stage is run.

With a common basis in every cell, this is algebraically equivalent to one
saturated regression using the block design
\[
q(X,D)=
\begin{pmatrix}
\mathbf 1\{D=1\}p_K(X)\\
\mathbf 1\{D=2\}p_K(X)\\
\mathbf 1\{D=3\}p_K(X)\\
\mathbf 1\{D=4\}p_K(X)
\end{pmatrix},
\]
because the Gram matrix is block diagonal. The separate-cell implementation is
more memory efficient and permits cell-specific diagnostics and, when requested,
cell-specific dimensions.

\subsection*{\code{midcdf}}

An optional numeric vector of conditional mid-CDF estimates, or a character
string naming a numeric column in \code{data}. Supplying it bypasses the entire
first-stage series estimation. It is useful for externally estimated nuisance
functions, cross-fitting, machine-learning first stages, or oracle exercises.

The vector is checked for finite values and for values outside \([0,1]\). The
\code{clip} rule is then applied in exactly the same way as for internally
estimated values.

\subsection*{\code{basis}}

Two bases are available:

\begin{description}[style=nextline,leftmargin=3.2cm]
\item[\code{"legendre"}]
An orthonormal Legendre-polynomial series on the normalized interval \([0,1]\).
For each continuous margin, the function uses the standard recurrence and
normalizes degree \(r\) by \(\sqrt{2r+1}\).

\item[\code{"spline"}]
A polynomial spline sieve represented internally by the numerically stable basis
returned by \code{splines::bs()}. Interior knots are equally spaced on the
normalized interval, boundary knots are \(0\) and \(1\), and
\code{intercept = TRUE}. Select this basis with \code{basis = "spline"}.
\end{description}

With two or more continuous variables, the marginal bases are combined by a
full row-wise tensor product.

\subsection*{\code{kappa}}

The exponent in the automatic sieve rule
\[
T=\left\lfloor c\,n_{\mathrm{ref}}^{\kappa}\right\rfloor,
\]
where \(c\) is \code{basis_constant}. The defaults are
\[
\kappa=0.245\quad\text{for Legendre},
\qquad
\kappa=0.300\quad\text{for splines}.
\]
The interpretation of \(T\) depends on \code{sieve_size_type}.

\subsection*{\code{basis_constant}}

The strictly positive multiplier \(c\) in the automatic rule. The default is
one. For example, \code{basis_constant = 1.5} replaces
\(\lfloor n_{\mathrm{ref}}^{\kappa}\rfloor\) by
\(\lfloor1.5n_{\mathrm{ref}}^{\kappa}\rfloor\).

\subsection*{\code{sieve_size}}

An optional fixed sieve size that replaces the automatic power rule.

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
\item Under \code{sieve_size_type = "dimension"}, it must be one positive
      scalar and is interpreted as a target total tensor-product dimension.
\item Under \code{sieve_size_type = "knots"}, it may be one nonnegative scalar,
      repeated over all continuous margins, or a nonnegative vector of length
      \code{length(continuous)} giving the marginal interior-knot counts
      directly.
\end{itemize}

For Legendre bases, \code{"knots"} is unavailable.

\subsection*{\code{sieve_size_type}}

Controls how the raw size \(T\) is interpreted.

\subsubsection*{\code{"auto"}}

The default is \code{"dimension"} for both bases: \(T\) is the target total
tensor-product dimension, which is the \(K\) of the paper, so that
\(T=\lfloor n_{\mathrm{ref}}^{\kappa}\rfloor\) grows at the rate assumed by the
theory. With cubic splines and one continuous regressor, \(T\) columns mean
\(J=T-4\) interior knots; for example \(T=6\) gives two interior knots.
An alternative convention applies \(\lfloor n^{0.3}\rfloor\) to the number of
interior knots instead, giving ten columns in that example; it is available
through \code{"knots"}.

For \(d\geq2\) continuous regressors, applying a knot rule in every margin
would give total tensor dimension
\[
K=\prod_{\ell=1}^{d}(J+s_0)=(J+s_0)^d,
\]
growing roughly as \(n^{0.3d}\), far faster than the rate conditions allow.
The default therefore controls the total dimension and allocates it across
margins as described next.

\subsubsection*{\code{"dimension"}}

The raw size \(T\) is a target for the complete tensor-product dimension
\[
K=\prod_{\ell=1}^{d}m_\ell.
\]
For splines of order \(s_0\),
\[
m_\ell=s_0+J_\ell,
\]
where \(J_\ell\) is the number of interior knots in margin \(\ell\).

The function starts at the minimum admissible marginal dimensions and adds one
column at a time. Among feasible increments, it favors a currently smaller
margin, so the allocation is approximately balanced. It stops when no
single-column increment can be made without exceeding the effective target.
The dimensions need not be exactly equal, and the achieved product need not be
exactly \(T\).

For cubic splines, \(s_0=4\). Examples are:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{rrrrr}
\toprule
\(d\) & target \(T\) & marginal dimensions \((m_\ell)\) & knots \((J_\ell)\) & actual \(K\)\\
\midrule
2 & 100 & \((10,10)\) & \((6,6)\) & 100\\
2 & 35  & \((7,5)\)   & \((3,1)\) & 35\\
2 & 50  & \((7,7)\)   & \((3,3)\) & 49\\
3 & 100 & \((5,5,4)\) & \((1,1,0)\) & 100\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

When several increments are tied, the order of the variables in
\code{continuous} breaks the tie. For example, with
\code{continuous = c("x1", "x2", "x3")} and target 100, the allocation
\((5,5,4)\) gives the first two variables one interior knot and the third none.

For a spline of order \(s_0\), the minimum possible full-tensor dimension is
\[
K_{\min}=s_0^d.
\]
With cubic splines, this equals \(4^d\). Hence a target below \(4^d\) cannot be
attained; the minimum basis is used provided the cell is large enough. For
example, with two continuous regressors and target 9, the minimum is
\(4\times4=16\).

For Legendre polynomials the minimum is \(K_{\min}=2^d\): every continuous
regressor enters at least linearly, so no variable is silently dropped from
the first stage. With three continuous regressors and target 6, the
allocation is \((2,2,2)\), eight columns. Whenever the minimum exceeds the
target, the diagnostics record \code{floored = TRUE}. Because
\(\lfloor n^{0.3}\rfloor\) reaches 16 only near \(n=10{,}000\) and 64 only near
\(n=1{,}000{,}000\), the default cubic spline basis with two or more continuous
regressors is the knot-free tensor product at most sample sizes; a lower
\code{spline_order} or a larger \code{basis_constant} gives more flexibility
at moderate \(n\).

\subsubsection*{\code{"knots"}}

This mode applies only to splines and directly controls marginal interior-knot
counts. A scalar is repeated in every margin. A vector permits an intentionally
unbalanced allocation. With the automatic rule it applies
\(\lfloor n_{\mathrm{ref}}^{\kappa}\rfloor\) to the knot count of every margin
rather than to the total dimension.

For example,
\begin{lstlisting}
sieve_size_type = "knots",
sieve_size = c(3L, 1L),
spline_order = 4L
\end{lstlisting}
requests
\[
(J_1,J_2)=(3,1),
\qquad
(m_1,m_2)=(7,5),
\qquad
K=35.
\]

\subsection*{\code{sieve_size_reference}}

Determines which sample size is inserted into the automatic rule. It does not
create a holdout sample and is unrelated to cross-fitting.

\begin{description}[style=nextline,leftmargin=2.5cm]
\item[\code{"cell"}]
Use the number \(n_g\) of observations in the current joint discrete cell. This
is the default. Unequal cell sizes can
therefore produce unequal requested dimensions.

\item[\code{"full"}]
Use the full estimation-sample size \(n\) in every cell. Every cell receives the
same nominal requested size, although sparse-cell feasibility caps can still
reduce the actual basis.
\end{description}

For example, suppose \(n=1000\), the four cell sizes are
\((100,200,300,400)\), \(\kappa=0.3\), and cubic splines under the default
total-dimension rule. With \code{"cell"}, the requested total dimensions are
\[
\bigl(\lfloor100^{0.3}\rfloor,
      \lfloor200^{0.3}\rfloor,
      \lfloor300^{0.3}\rfloor,
      \lfloor400^{0.3}\rfloor\bigr)
=(3,4,5,6),
\]
so the first two cells use the four-column minimum (no interior knots) and the
others five and six columns (one and two interior knots).
With \code{"full"}, every cell requests
\[
\lfloor1000^{0.3}\rfloor=7
\]
columns, three interior knots.

\subsection*{\code{spline_order}}

The spline order \(s_0\), required to be an integer of at least two. Polynomial
degree equals \(s_0-1\). The default \code{spline_order = 4L} therefore gives a
cubic spline basis. With \(J_\ell\) interior knots and
\code{intercept = TRUE}, the marginal dimension is
\[
m_\ell=J_\ell+s_0.
\]

\subsection*{\code{basis_bounds}}

Bounds used to map each continuous first-stage variable to \([0,1]\). If
\code{NULL}, the function uses the minimum and maximum in the final estimation
sample. A named list is recommended:
\begin{lstlisting}
basis_bounds = list(
  x1 = c(0.1, 1),
  age = c(18, 65)
)
\end{lstlisting}
All observed values must lie inside the supplied bounds. Scaling is performed
using the full estimation sample before it is divided into discrete cells, so
every cell uses the same coordinate transformation.

Known support bounds are preferable whenever the support is known, for
example in a simulation study. The
bootstrap retains those bounds and the corresponding basis map. Linear Gini
prediction uses the second-stage design, not a new first-stage CDF evaluation.

\subsection*{\code{clip}}

A logical value controlling whether first-stage fitted values outside
\([0,1]\) are truncated.

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
\item \code{FALSE} (default): retain the linear-series fitted values and warn;
\item \code{TRUE}: warn and replace values below zero by zero and values above
      one by one.
\end{itemize}

Clipping changes \(2\mhat_i-1\) and therefore changes the GIR point estimate. It
is not merely a display operation.

\subsection*{\code{first_stage_singular}}

Specifies how a singular or ill-conditioned first-stage cell is handled. The
function diagnoses each cell using the reciprocal condition number of the Gram
matrix and the QR rank of the weighted basis matrix.

\begin{description}[style=nextline,leftmargin=2.5cm]
\item[\code{"error"}]
Stop and identify the cells that need attention. This default preserves the
unregularized first-stage specification.

\item[\code{"ridge"}]
Replace the cell Gram matrix by
\[
G_g+\lambda_g I,
\qquad
\lambda_g=\code{ridge}\times
\operatorname{mean}(\operatorname{diag}G_g).
\]
There is no absolute floor on the matrix scale. The applied value is stored in
the diagnostics. If the solve fails, a further \(100\lambda_g\) increment is
attempted. The total is \(101\lambda_g\) if the initial penalty was already
applied, or \(100\lambda_g\) if only the retry was needed. A successful retry
is reported as \code{action = "ridge"} in either case.

Multiplying all weights by \(c>0\) multiplies \(G_g\), its right-hand side, and
the penalty by \(c\), preserving the fitted values up to floating-point error
within usable numerical ranges. Nonpositive or nonfinite matrix scales,
penalties that underflow or overflow, and nonfinite adjusted matrices are
rejected with informative errors.

\item[\code{"drop"}]
Use pivoted QR to identify a numerically independent subset of basis columns,
remove the remaining columns, and report their names. The resulting effective
sieve need not remain a complete tensor product.

\item[\code{"none"}]
Apply no proactive correction and pass the unmodified Gram matrix to
\code{solve()}. A numerical failure remains an error.
\end{description}

\subsection*{\code{first_stage_tol}}

The tolerance used in first-stage reciprocal-condition and QR-rank diagnostics.
The default is \(10^{-12}\). A cell is marked problematic when its reciprocal
condition number is nonfinite or below the tolerance, or when its numerical rank
is below the number of basis columns.

\subsection*{\code{ridge}}

The relative ridge constant. It must be strictly positive when
\code{first_stage_singular = "ridge"}; zero is allowed when ridge is not used.
The default is \(10^{-10}\), a small numerical stabilization heuristic, not a
universal regression default, a cross-validated choice, or a value prescribed
by the paper's theory. A small penalty can still materially affect directions
that the data identify poorly. Inspect the diagnostics and assess sensitivity
to the ridge constant when using this option. The overall default remains
\code{first_stage_singular = "error"}, which does not apply ridge.

Earlier versions imposed a floor of 1 on the average Gram diagonal. Removing
that floor can change both point estimates and bootstrap inference when ridge
is active and the average diagonal is below 1. Affected analyses should refit
and rerun their bootstrap. Ridge regularization remains a modification of the
first-stage estimator requiring its own statistical justification.

\subsection*{\code{minimum_cell_size}}

The minimum number of positive-weight observations required in every observed
joint discrete cell. The default is eight. A failure names the cell and reports
both its total row count and positive-weight row count.

\subsection*{\code{max_basis_columns}}

A safety cap for the number of columns in a full tensor-product basis. The
actual cell-specific upper bound is
\[
\min\{\code{max_basis_columns},\ n_{+,g}-2\},
\]
where \(n_{+,g}\) is the number of positive-weight observations in cell \(g\).
The default package cap is 1000 columns.

\subsection*{\code{singular.ok}}

Controls second-stage rank deficiency in the style of \code{lm()}.

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
\item \code{FALSE}: a singular second-stage moment matrix is an error;
\item \code{TRUE}: pivoted QR retains an estimable subset, aliased coefficients
      are returned as \code{NA}, and a warning is issued.
\end{itemize}

The default is \code{TRUE}. A full-rank but ill-conditioned matrix is not
regularized; the function returns the unregularized estimate and warns.

\subsection*{\code{second_stage_tol}}

The QR-rank and reciprocal-condition tolerance for the second stage. The
default is \(10^{-7}\).

\subsection*{\code{GIR}}

A logical value. When \code{TRUE}, compute the GIR-specific goodness-of-fit
measure based on the estimated conditional centered mid-rank. Let
\(z_i=2\mhat_i-1\), let \(\widehat z_i=X_i'\thetahat\), and let
\[
\widehat a_0
=
\frac{\sum_iw_iY_i z_i}{\sum_iw_iY_i}.
\]
Then
\[
\RtwoG
=
1-
\frac{\sum_iw_iY_i(z_i-\widehat z_i)^2}
     {\sum_iw_iY_i(z_i-\widehat a_0)^2}.
\]
When the unrestricted formula contains an intercept and the weighted GIR loss
is nonnegative, the constant-only model is nested. Without an intercept, the
function warns that the usual \([0,1]\) interpretation need not hold.

\subsection*{\code{model}, \code{x}, and \code{y}}

Logical retention controls:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
\item \code{model = TRUE}: retain the second-stage model frame;
\item \code{x = TRUE}: retain the second-stage design matrix;
\item \code{y = TRUE}: retain the response vector.
\end{itemize}

The flags govern these public slots. To support reproducible bootstrap refits,
the fitted object also retains an \code{estimation} snapshot
containing the distinct design rows with the index that maps observations to
them, the response, weights, first-stage data and controls, irrespective of
these three flags. Setting them to \code{FALSE} does not remove all underlying
data from the object or make it anonymous. Observations with the same
regressor values share a design row, so with cell-level regressors (industry
and year effects in firm data, for example) the snapshot holds one row per
cell rather than one per observation; \code{x = TRUE} expands it to the full
design.

\subsection*{\code{contrasts}}

An optional contrast specification passed to \code{model.matrix()} for the
second-stage formula.

\section{First-stage dimension allocation in detail}

\subsection{One continuous variable}

When \(d=1\), there is no nontrivial tensor-product multiplication. The marginal
dimension equals the total dimension.

Under the default total-dimension rule with \(n_{\mathrm{ref}}=500\),
\[
K=\lfloor500^{0.3}\rfloor=6,
\qquad
J_1=K-4=2,
\]
a cubic spline with two interior knots and six columns. The alternative
\code{"knots"} convention applies the same number to the knot count instead,
\[
J_1=6,
\qquad
m_1=J_1+4=10,
\qquad
K=m_1=10.
\]

\subsection{Two or more continuous variables}

For \(d\geq2\), the total basis is the row-wise tensor product. If the marginal
dimensions are \((m_1,\ldots,m_d)\), then
\[
K=\prod_{\ell=1}^{d}m_\ell.
\]
Under \code{"dimension"}, the allocation algorithm chooses the
\((m_1,\ldots,m_d)\) vector. Under \code{"knots"}, the user or automatic rule
chooses \((J_1,\ldots,J_d)\), after which
\(m_\ell=J_\ell+s_0\).

The balanced allocation is a deterministic implementation convention; it does
not infer variable importance. When a tie is resolved in favor of an earlier
variable, changing the order of \code{continuous} can change which margin gets
the final additional column.

\section{Discrete cells and basis-size reference}

With both continuous and discrete first-stage variables, the default
\code{sieve_size_reference = "cell"} generally does \emph{not} force the same
basis dimension in every cell. If cell sizes differ, the raw rules
\[
\left\lfloor c n_g^\kappa\right\rfloor
\]
can differ. Feasibility capping or singularity handling can create further
differences.

To request a common nominal automatic size, use
\code{sieve_size_reference = "full"}. To request a common fixed size, provide
\code{sieve_size}. Even then, the actual basis can be reduced in a sparse cell,
and \code{first_stage_singular = "drop"} can lower the effective dimension.
Always inspect the diagnostics rather than inferring the realized dimensions
from the call alone.

\section{Diagnostics}

For an internally estimated first stage, use
\begin{lstlisting}
d <- fit$first_stage$diagnostics
\end{lstlisting}
The data frame has one row per observed joint discrete cell. It contains the
following fields.

\begin{longtable}{>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.30\textwidth}p{0.64\textwidth}}
\toprule
Field & Meaning\\
\midrule
\endhead
\code{cell} & Human-readable joint discrete-cell label.\\
\code{observations} & Number of rows in the cell.\\
\code{positive_weight_}\newline\code{observations} & Number of rows with strictly positive observation weight.\\
\code{requested_size} & Raw requested scalar or knot vector before cell feasibility adjustments.\\
\code{size_type} & Realized interpretation: \code{"dimension"}, \code{"knots"}, or \code{"constant"}.\\
\code{marginal_dimensions} & A list-column. Each row contains a named integer vector \((m_1,\ldots,m_d)\), with names equal to \code{continuous}. These are the marginal basis dimensions actually constructed after size allocation and capping, but before optional QR column dropping.\\
\code{marginal_knot_counts} & A list-column. For \code{basis = "spline"}, each row contains the named integer vector \((J_1,\ldots,J_d)\). For Legendre bases, it contains a named vector of \code{NA_integer_} values because knot counts are not applicable.\\
\code{basis_dimension} & Total number of constructed tensor-product columns. With continuous variables, this equals \(\prod_\ell m_\ell\); with none, it equals one.\\
\code{effective_dimension} & Number of columns used after singularity handling. It differs from \code{basis_dimension} when \code{first_stage_singular = "drop"}.\\
\code{rank} & Numerical rank reported for the cell fit.\\
\code{rcond} & Reciprocal condition number of the original cell Gram matrix. Smaller values indicate worse conditioning.\\
\code{singular} & Whether the original cell design was diagnosed as singular or ill-conditioned.\\
\code{action} & \code{"none"}, \code{"ridge"}, or \code{"drop"}.\\
\code{ridge_lambda} & Actual ridge amount added to the Gram diagonal.\\
\code{dropped_columns} & Semicolon-separated names of columns removed by pivoted QR.\\
\code{capped} & Whether the requested size was reduced by a cell-specific feasibility limit.\\
\code{floored} & Whether the minimum tensor dimension (\(2^d\) for Legendre, \(s_0^d\) for splines) exceeded the requested size, so the minimum was used.\\
\bottomrule
\end{longtable}

The list-columns preserve named integer vectors rather than flattening them to
text. Typical extraction is:
\begin{lstlisting}
# Complete vector in the first cell
d$marginal_dimensions[[1L]]
d$marginal_knot_counts[[1L]]

# Stack all cells into ordinary matrices
marginal_dimensions_by_cell <- do.call(
  rbind,
  d$marginal_dimensions
)

marginal_knots_by_cell <- do.call(
  rbind,
  d$marginal_knot_counts
)
\end{lstlisting}

For example, a cubic two-dimensional spline cell with
\begin{lstlisting}
d$marginal_dimensions[[1L]]
# x1 x2
#  7  5

d$marginal_knot_counts[[1L]]
# x1 x2
#  3  1
\end{lstlisting}
has
\[
K=7\times5=35.
\]

If pivoted QR subsequently drops three interaction columns, the diagnostics can
show
\[
\code{basis_dimension}=35,
\qquad
\code{effective_dimension}=32.
\]
There need not exist a meaningful effective marginal vector whose product is
32, because QR may remove individual tensor interactions rather than an entire
marginal function. The reported marginal vectors therefore describe the
constructed tensor basis before column dropping.

\section{Pivoted QR and aliasing}

For a matrix \(P\), an ordinary QR decomposition writes
\[
P=QR,
\]
where \(Q\) has orthonormal columns and \(R\) is upper triangular. A
column-pivoted QR decomposition writes
\[
P\Pi=QR,
\]
where \(\Pi\) permutes the columns. The algorithm attempts to move informative,
linearly independent columns first and redundant columns last.

For example, the design
\[
P=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf 1 & x & 2x\end{bmatrix}
\]
has three columns but rank two because its third column is twice its second.
Pivoted QR retains two independent columns and identifies one aliased column.

In the first stage, \code{"drop"} applies pivoted QR to the weighted design
\(\operatorname{diag}(\sqrt w)P\). In the second stage,
\code{singular.ok = TRUE} uses the same principle to report estimable
coefficients and return \code{NA} for aliased ones.

Rank deficiency and ill-conditioning are not identical. A matrix may be full
rank but nearly singular. The GIR package therefore records both QR rank and reciprocal
condition number.

\section{Value}

An object of class
\begin{lstlisting}
c("gir", "gir.object")
\end{lstlisting}
with principal components:

\begin{longtable}{>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.31\textwidth}p{0.63\textwidth}}
\toprule
Component & Contents\\
\midrule
\endhead
\code{coefficients} & Named point estimates, with aliased entries set to \code{NA}.\\
\code{coefficients.complete} & A complete coefficient vector used for fitted values, with zero in aliased positions.\\
\code{fitted.values} & Fitted linear conditional Gini index \(X_i'\widehat\theta\); equivalently the fitted index in the generated mid-rank regression.\\
\code{residuals} & \((2\widehat M_i-1)-X_i'\widehat\theta\).\\
\code{rank}, \code{df.residual}, \code{aliased} & Second-stage rank information.\\
\code{midcdf} & Named vector \((\widehat M_1,\ldots,\widehat M_n)\).\\
\code{centered.midrank} & Named vector \(2\widehat M_i-1\).\\
\code{GIR.R2} & \code{NULL} unless \code{GIR = TRUE}; otherwise a list containing the value, restricted coefficient, unrestricted loss, and restricted loss.\\
\code{first_stage} & First-stage specification, bounds, cell factor, clipping information, singularity settings, and diagnostics.\\
\code{second_stage} & Singularity indicators, reciprocal condition number, tolerance, moment matrix, and moment vector.\\
\code{call}, \code{formula}, \code{terms}, \code{contrasts}, \code{xlevels}, \code{assign} & Formula and design metadata.\\
\code{weights} & Final estimation-sample weights.\\
\code{na.action} & Omission information, when applicable.\\
\code{nobs}, \code{original.nobs}, \code{subset.nobs}, \code{row.index} & Sample-size and row-alignment information.\\
\code{model}, \code{x}, \code{y} & Retained model frame, design, and response according to the retention arguments.\\
\code{estimation} & Resolved estimation snapshot for refitting and prediction, retained independently of the public-slot flags: the distinct design rows, the index mapping observations to them, response, weights, first-stage data and controls.\\
\bottomrule
\end{longtable}

\section{Methods}

The current object supports:
\begin{lstlisting}
print(fit)
summary(fit)
coef(fit)
fitted(fit)
residuals(fit)
weights(fit)
nobs(fit)
formula(fit)
terms(fit)
model.frame(fit)
model.matrix(fit)
midcdf(fit)
summary(midcdf(fit))
\end{lstlisting}

\code{summary(fit)} remains the point-estimator summary and prints a one-column
coefficient table. Use \code{summary(bboot.gir(fit, ...))} for inference; the
point object is not silently refitted by its summary method. \code{summary(midcdf(fit))} uses the ordinary numeric
vector summary and reports the minimum, quartiles, median, mean, and maximum.

\section{Warnings and errors}

\subsection*{Errors}

The function stops for, among other cases:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
\item missing data under the default \code{na.fail};
\item empty input, an empty subset, or no observations after missing-value handling;
\item nonnumeric or nonfinite response values;
\item negative or infinite weights, or no positive weight in the retained sample;
\item first-stage columns not found in \code{data};
\item overlap between \code{continuous} and \code{discrete};
\item an undeclared numeric first-stage column with at most two distinct values;
\item observed continuous values outside \code{basis_bounds};
\item a sparse joint discrete cell;
\item a cell too small for the minimum tensor basis (\(2^d\) Legendre or
      \(s_0^d\) spline columns);
\item first-stage singularity under \code{"error"}, including a marginal
      basis with more columns than its variable has distinct values in a cell;
\item a failed unregularized solve under \code{"none"};
\item second-stage singularity when \code{singular.ok = FALSE}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Warnings}

The function warns when:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
\item one or more response values are strictly negative;
\item observations are removed by \code{na.omit} or \code{na.exclude};
\item a custom action removes rows (with separate counts for missing and complete rows);
\item first-stage fitted values fall outside \([0,1]\), whether or not clipped;
\item a requested first-stage basis is capped;
\item first-stage singularity is handled by ridge or column dropping,
      including the case of a marginal basis larger than its variable's
      support, which is identified in the warning;
\item the second-stage design is rank deficient or ill-conditioned;
\item \(R_G^2\) is undefined or requested without an intercept.
\end{itemize}

A negative response does not mechanically prevent calculation of the moment
estimator, but it undermines the usual Gini interpretation and the ordinary
nonnegative-loss interpretation of \(R_G^2\).

\section{Examples}

The examples below assume that the package has been installed and attached
with \code{library(GIR)}.

\subsection{Basic fit with one continuous and two binary first-stage variables}

\begin{lstlisting}
fit <- gir(
  y ~ x1 + x2 + x3,
  data = dat,
  first_stage = ~ x1 + x2 + x3,
  continuous = "x1",
  discrete = c("x2", "x3"),
  basis = "legendre",
  GIR = TRUE
)

summary(fit)
summary(midcdf(fit))
fit$first_stage$diagnostics
\end{lstlisting}

The two binary variables generate up to four observed cells. The Legendre basis
is estimated separately in each cell.

\subsection{A numeric discrete variable with four values}

\begin{lstlisting}
fit4 <- gir(
  y ~ x + factor(d),
  data = dat4,
  first_stage = ~ x + d,
  continuous = "x",
  discrete = "d",          # important: d is numeric
  basis = "spline",
  spline_order = 4L
)

levels(fit4$first_stage$cells)
fit4$first_stage$diagnostics
\end{lstlisting}

If \code{d} takes \(1,2,3,4\), the first-stage diagnostic table has one row per
observed value. The second-stage formula uses \code{factor(d)} to avoid imposing
a linear effect in the numerical labels.

\subsection{A Legendre specification with known support bounds}

\begin{lstlisting}
fit_legendre <- gir(
  y ~ 0 + x1 + x2 + x3,
  data = dat,
  first_stage = ~ x1 + x2 + x3,
  continuous = "x1",
  discrete = c("x2", "x3"),
  basis = "legendre",
  kappa = 0.245,
  basis_constant = 1,
  sieve_size_type = "dimension",
  sieve_size_reference = "cell",
  basis_bounds = list(x1 = c(0.1, 1)),
  clip = FALSE,
  first_stage_singular = "ridge",
  first_stage_tol = 1e-12,
  ridge = 1e-10,
  singular.ok = FALSE
)
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{A cubic spline specification with knots per margin}

\begin{lstlisting}
fit_spline <- gir(
  y ~ 0 + x1 + x2 + x3,
  data = dat,
  first_stage = ~ x1 + x2 + x3,
  continuous = "x1",
  discrete = c("x2", "x3"),
  basis = "spline",
  kappa = 0.300,
  basis_constant = 1,
  sieve_size_type = "knots",
  sieve_size_reference = "cell",
  spline_order = 4L,
  basis_bounds = list(x1 = c(0.1, 1)),
  clip = FALSE,
  first_stage_singular = "ridge",
  first_stage_tol = 1e-12,
  ridge = 1e-10,
  singular.ok = FALSE
)
\end{lstlisting}

Here \code{sieve_size_type = "knots"} makes \(\lfloor n_g^{0.3}\rfloor\) the
number of interior knots in cell \(g\) rather than the total dimension. With
one continuous regressor, the cell's marginal and total dimensions equal the
knot count plus four.

\subsection{Target total dimension with two continuous regressors}

\begin{lstlisting}
fit_35 <- gir(
  y ~ x1 + x2,
  data = dat2,
  first_stage = ~ x1 + x2,
  continuous = c("x1", "x2"),
  basis = "spline",
  spline_order = 4L,
  sieve_size_type = "dimension",
  sieve_size = 35L,
  first_stage_singular = "ridge"
)

fit_35$first_stage$diagnostics$marginal_dimensions[[1L]]
# x1 x2
#  7  5

fit_35$first_stage$diagnostics$marginal_knot_counts[[1L]]
# x1 x2
#  3  1
\end{lstlisting}

Provided the cell-size cap does not bind, the tensor dimension is
\(7\times5=35\). With target 50, the balance-oriented path returns
\((7,7)\), hence 49 columns, rather than searching globally for the unbalanced
factorization \(10\times5=50\).

\subsection{Directly specify marginal spline knots}

\begin{lstlisting}
fit_knots <- gir(
  y ~ x1 + x2,
  data = dat2,
  first_stage = ~ x1 + x2,
  continuous = c("x1", "x2"),
  basis = "spline",
  spline_order = 4L,
  sieve_size_type = "knots",
  sieve_size = c(3L, 1L)
)
\end{lstlisting}

This explicitly requests marginal dimensions \((7,5)\) and total dimension 35.
A scalar \code{sieve_size = 3L} would request three knots in both margins and
total dimension \(7^2=49\).

\subsection{Compare cell and full reference sizes}

\begin{lstlisting}
fit_cell <- gir(
  y ~ x + factor(d), data = dat4,
  first_stage = ~ x + d,
  continuous = "x", discrete = "d",
  basis = "spline",
  sieve_size_reference = "cell"
)

fit_full <- gir(
  y ~ x + factor(d), data = dat4,
  first_stage = ~ x + d,
  continuous = "x", discrete = "d",
  basis = "spline",
  sieve_size_reference = "full"
)

fit_cell$first_stage$diagnostics[
  c("cell", "requested_size", "marginal_dimensions")
]

fit_full$first_stage$diagnostics[
  c("cell", "requested_size", "marginal_dimensions")
]
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Supply the first-stage estimates}

\begin{lstlisting}
fit_external <- gir(
  y ~ treatment + x1 + x2,
  data = dat,
  midcdf = external_midcdf,
  clip = FALSE,
  singular.ok = FALSE
)

# A data-column name is also accepted.
fit_external_2 <- gir(
  y ~ treatment + x1 + x2,
  data = dat,
  midcdf = "external_midcdf"
)
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Weights and missing values}

\begin{lstlisting}
fit_weighted <- gir(
  y ~ treatment + x,
  data = dat,
  weights = survey_weight,
  na.action = stats::na.omit,
  first_stage = ~ treatment + x,
  continuous = "x",
  discrete = "treatment",
  basis = "spline"
)
\end{lstlisting}

Rows incomplete in any required object are removed together, and the same
weights enter both stages.

\subsection{Inspect clipping}

\begin{lstlisting}
fit_unclipped <- gir(
  y ~ x, data = dat,
  continuous = "x",
  basis = "legendre",
  clip = FALSE
)

fit_clipped <- gir(
  y ~ x, data = dat,
  continuous = "x",
  basis = "legendre",
  clip = TRUE
)

range(midcdf(fit_unclipped))
range(midcdf(fit_clipped))
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{First-stage singularity policies}

\begin{lstlisting}
fit_strict <- gir(
  y ~ x, data = dat,
  continuous = "x",
  basis = "spline",
  first_stage_singular = "error"
)

fit_ridge <- gir(
  y ~ x, data = dat,
  continuous = "x",
  basis = "spline",
  first_stage_singular = "ridge",
  ridge = 1e-10
)

fit_drop <- gir(
  y ~ x, data = dat,
  continuous = "x",
  basis = "spline",
  first_stage_singular = "drop"
)
\end{lstlisting}

After a ridge or drop fit, inspect
\begin{lstlisting}
fit_ridge$first_stage$diagnostics[
  c("cell", "singular", "action", "ridge_lambda")
]

fit_drop$first_stage$diagnostics[
  c("cell", "basis_dimension", "effective_dimension",
    "action", "dropped_columns")
]
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Strict second-stage singularity behavior}

\begin{lstlisting}
fit <- gir(
  y ~ x1 + x2,
  data = dat,
  singular.ok = FALSE
)
\end{lstlisting}

This stops rather than returning aliased coefficients when the GIR moment matrix
is singular.

\subsection{GIR goodness of fit}

\begin{lstlisting}
fit_r2 <- gir(
  y ~ treatment + x1 + x2,
  data = dat,
  first_stage = ~ treatment + x1 + x2,
  continuous = c("x1", "x2"),
  discrete = "treatment",
  basis = "spline",
  GIR = TRUE
)

summary(fit_r2)
fit_r2$GIR.R2
\end{lstlisting}

\section{\texorpdfstring{\code{bboot.gir}: Bayesian bootstrap inference}{bboot.gir: Bayesian bootstrap inference}}
\label{sec:bootstrap}

\subsection{Name and description}

\code{bboot.gir()} obtains Bayesian-bootstrap coefficient draws from a fitted
linear GIR model. In its default mode it re-estimates both the conditional
mid-distribution first stage and the GIR second stage using the same
mean-one exponential multipliers. It reports the basic confidence intervals
in Algorithm 2 of the paper, bootstrap standard errors and covariance,
bootstrap bias estimates, and centered-bootstrap hypothesis tests.

The interval construction and two-stage resampling scheme are taken from
Algorithm 2 of the paper \cite{girpaper}. Exact ties use the
mid-distribution response of the paper's first stage.
The standard errors, smooth transformations, marginal tests, joint Wald test
and prediction standard errors are package extensions; their assumptions
and calculations are described below.

\subsection{Usage}

\begin{lstlisting}
bboot.gir(
  object,
  R = 999L,
  level = 0.95,
  ...,
  transform = NULL,
  null = 0,
  alternative = c("two.sided", "less", "greater"),
  joint = FALSE,
  seed = NULL,
  cluster = NULL,
  cluster_order = c("auto", "original", "estimation"),
  bootstrap_weights = NULL,
  normalize_weights = TRUE,
  first_stage_boot = c("refit", "fixed"),
  midcdf_boot = NULL,
  inference = TRUE,
  keep_weights = FALSE,
  keep_midcdf = FALSE,
  failure = c("error", "omit")
)
\end{lstlisting}

The preferred workflow separates estimation from inference:
\begin{lstlisting}
fit <- gir(y ~ x + factor(d), data = dat,
           first_stage = ~ x + d,
           continuous = "x", discrete = "d")
bb <- bboot.gir(fit, R = 999L, level = 0.95, seed = 42L)
summary(bb)
confint(bb)
vcov(bb)
\end{lstlisting}
Here and below, examples using an unspecified \code{dat} illustrate syntax;
Section~\ref{sec:worked} supplies a complete runnable data-generating example.

\subsection{Bootstrapping a fitted model}

The fitted object identifies the actual estimation sample after \code{subset}
and missing-value processing, the second-stage columns and contrasts, the
continuous/discrete first-stage classification, and all selected cell-specific
basis dimensions. Repeating a call against a subsequently modified data frame
could otherwise change the estimator being bootstrapped.

A \code{gir} fit retains the data and settings used for estimation.
\code{bboot.gir(fit)} uses that snapshot, not the possibly changed data named in
\code{fit$call}. It does not allow extra \code{gir()} arguments when a fitted
object is supplied. To change the basis or formula, fit the new point estimator
first, then bootstrap that fit.

A formula convenience form is also available:
\begin{lstlisting}
bb <- bboot.gir(
  y ~ x + factor(d),
  data = dat,
  first_stage = ~ x + d,
  continuous = "x", discrete = "d",
  basis = "spline", spline_order = 4L,
  singular.ok = FALSE,
  R = 999L, level = 0.95, seed = 42L
)
fit <- bb$fit
\end{lstlisting}
In this form, the first argument is the formula, not a fitted model. The
additional, fully named arguments are passed to \code{gir()} once. Expressions
for \code{subset}, \code{weights}, and a supplied \code{midcdf} are evaluated
through the same data-based machinery as in a direct \code{gir()} call.
\code{R}, \code{level}, and the other explicit bootstrap arguments remain
bootstrap options, not point-estimator arguments.

\subsection{Arguments: model, replicates and confidence level}

\paragraph{\code{object}.}
Supply a fit created by \code{gir()}. A two-sided formula
is accepted as the convenience form above. An older saved fit without the
estimation snapshot must be refitted with the current function. A fitted
second-stage model with aliased coefficients is not accepted for bootstrap
coefficient inference: use an identified, full-rank specification.

\paragraph{\code{R}.}
The positive integer number of requested bootstrap replicates. Its default is
999. This is denoted by \(B\) in the mathematical formulas below. If a
\code{bootstrap_weights} matrix is supplied and \code{R} is omitted, its number
of columns determines \code{R}; an explicitly supplied inconsistent value is
an error. With \code{inference=TRUE}, at least two successful draws are required.
The choice \code{R=1, inference=FALSE} provides a single draw, as used in a
warp-speed Monte Carlo study; it does not provide a usable sample-specific
standard error or confidence interval.

\paragraph{\code{level}.}
A scalar strictly between zero and one; the default is 0.95. The confidence
intervals are two-sided, equal-tailed basic bootstrap intervals at that level.
Changing the test's \code{alternative} does not change these intervals into
one-sided bounds. A different interval level can be obtained from stored draws
with \code{confint(bb, level=0.90)} or \code{summary(bb, level=0.90)}.

For a two-sided level \(1-\alpha\), only about \(B\alpha/2\) draws inform
each tail. The summary warns when this number is below ten. This is a numerical
precision diagnostic, not a theorem about a universally adequate value of
\(B\). The small \(B\) values used in unit tests and algebraic illustrations
are not recommended for reported empirical tail inference.

\subsection{Arguments: transformations and hypotheses}

\paragraph{\code{transform}.}
Defines \(h(\theta)\), the statistic or vector of statistics to test. It does
not replace the raw coefficients stored in \code{t0} and \code{t}.
The accepted forms are:
\begin{description}[style=nextline,leftmargin=1.8cm]
\item[\code{NULL}]
Use all coefficients, in their original order. With \code{null=0}, this gives
separate coefficient-zero tests by default.
\item[Numeric vector]
An unnamed vector must have one entry per coefficient and defines a single
linear contrast. A named vector can select a subset of coefficient names; all
unmentioned coefficients receive weight zero. For example,
\code{c(x2=1, x3=-1)} defines \(h(\theta)=\theta_{x2}-\theta_{x3}\).
The resulting statistic is named \code{contrast}.
\item[Numeric matrix]
Each row defines a linear contrast. An unnamed matrix must have one column
per coefficient. Named columns may be supplied in any order and may select a
subset; omitted coefficient columns are filled with zero. Row names label the
statistics; absent row names are generated as \code{contrast1}, and so on.
\item[Function]
A deterministic function of the named coefficient vector. It must return a
finite numeric vector of the same length and with the same names for the point
estimate and every successful draw. An unnamed result is assigned names
\code{statistic1}, and so on. Undefined ratios, logs, or other nonfinite values
cause an error rather than selective deletion of transformed draws.
\end{description}

The intended theoretical class is smooth transformations with a nondegenerate
first derivative at the true parameter. The numerical interface does not verify
this mathematical condition. In particular, \(h(\theta)=\theta_j^2\) when
\(\theta_{0j}=0\), absolute values at zero, maxima at ties, and ratios with
nearly zero denominators may be nonregular; obtaining numeric output does not
establish bootstrap validity for them. For joint inference, the derivative
matrix of the restrictions must have full row rank at the null. A necessary
check, enforced in the code, is that the number of restrictions does not exceed
the number of coefficients; that check is not sufficient on its own.

\paragraph{\code{null}.}
The hypothesized value \(r\) in \(H_0:h(\theta_0)=r\). An unnamed scalar is
repeated for all transformed components. An unnamed vector must have exactly
one entry per component. A named vector must match all transformed statistic
names exactly and is reordered accordingly. The default is zero. For a
coefficient ratio equal to one, supply \code{null=1}; for a log-ratio equal to
zero, supply \code{null=0}.

\paragraph{\code{alternative}.}
\code{"two.sided"} uses twice the smaller centered-bootstrap tail probability,
capped at one. \code{"less"} tests against \(h_j(\theta_0)<r_j\), and
\code{"greater"} against \(h_j(\theta_0)>r_j\). The same alternative applies
to each component. Marginal p-values are not adjusted for multiple testing.

\paragraph{\code{joint}.}
If \code{TRUE}, also compute the centered, common-covariance bootstrap Wald
test of all supplied restrictions jointly. It requires
\code{alternative="two.sided"}. This is not the ordinary OLS F test, and
it does not use a Student or F reference distribution. A singular or numerically
ill-conditioned bootstrap restriction covariance yields an explicit warning
and an undefined joint statistic/p-value, not an arbitrary generalized-inverse
test. Remove redundant restrictions or obtain more draws as appropriate.

For example, testing both \(\theta_2-\theta_3=0\) and
\(\theta_2/\theta_3=1\) jointly is redundant when the denominator is nonzero.
Use one of these equivalent restrictions, not both as independent restrictions.

\subsection{Arguments: random weights and reproducibility}

\paragraph{\code{seed}.}
\code{NULL} uses and advances the caller's random-number stream. A nonnegative
integer initializes a local seeded computation and restores both the caller's
RNG kind and its previous \code{.Random.seed} on exit, including error exits.
A supplied seed and supplied bootstrap multipliers cannot be used together.


\paragraph{\code{cluster}.}
Optional clustering of the multipliers. A one-sided formula such as
\code{~ industry} names retained model-frame or first-stage columns, whose
combination defines the clusters (\code{~ industry + region} clusters on the
intersection); a vector supplies one cluster value per estimation row, or one
per row of the original data. The \code{cluster_order} argument below determines
how a vector is aligned. For each replicate one \(\mathrm{Exp}(1)\) value is drawn
per cluster and shared by all its observations in both stages, after which
the vector is normalized by its observation-level mean as usual. This is the
Bayesian cluster bootstrap and is the appropriate choice when observations are
dependent within groups, for example firms within industries when the
treatment varies at the industry level. It cannot be combined with
\code{bootstrap_weights}; a supplied multiplier matrix already fixes the
dependence structure.

Each distinct combination of values defines a cluster. Group membership uses
the values in each column, so labels such as \code{("a.b", "c")} and
\code{("a", "b.c")} remain distinct. Numeric identifiers are distinguished
by their stored values rather than their printed formatting. A formula such as
\code{~ industry + region} specifies one-way clustering at industry-region
intersections, not two-way clustering by industry and region separately.
Clusters are numbered in order of first occurrence in the estimation rows;
changing labels while preserving group membership preserves seeded multipliers.

When every first-stage cell lies inside a single cluster, the cell fits are
invariant to the common multiplier of that cluster, so the first stage does
not vary across draws and the bootstrap distribution reflects between-cluster
variation only. The estimated first stage then enters the inference through
the residual variation across clusters, exactly as in a clustered regression
of cell Gini indices; the returned \code{cluster$first_stage_invariant}
reports whether this is the case. The paper's validity theorem covers
independent observations; clustered inference rests on the usual
many-clusters argument and is the researcher's responsibility.

\paragraph{\code{cluster_order}.}
Specifies the row order of a cluster vector:
\begin{itemize}
\item \code{"original"} requires one label per original-data row, in the order
at the time \code{gir()} was called. The vector is aligned to the final
estimation sample using \code{fit$row.index}, including subsetting,
reordering, repeated selections, and removal of incomplete observations.
\item \code{"estimation"} requires one label per final estimation row and
uses the vector unchanged. For example, a vector already constructed as
\code{dat$id[fit$row.index]} has this order.
\item \code{"auto"} (the default) infers the order when the vector length
matches only one of the original-data and estimation-sample sizes. If both
sizes match, it accepts the vector only when the row mapping is unchanged.
Otherwise it stops and asks for an explicit order, since length alone cannot
distinguish original-order labels from already-aligned labels.
\end{itemize}
Missing cluster labels are checked after alignment. A missing label on a
discarded original row is harmless; a missing label on a used row is rejected.
Non-default values require a cluster vector. Leave \code{"auto"} for a cluster
formula, whose retained columns are already aligned, or for \code{cluster = NULL}.

For example, keeping all six observations in a different order requires an
explicit choice for a vector. The following computes draws only to illustrate
alignment:
\begin{verbatim}
cluster_dat <- data.frame(y = c(1, 4, 2, 7, 3, 9),
                          id = c("A", "A", "B", "B", "C", "C"))
cluster_fit <- gir(y ~ 1, data = cluster_dat,
                   subset = c(1, 3, 5, 2, 4, 6), first_stage = ~ id,
                   discrete = "id", minimum_cell_size = 1L)
cluster_boot <- bboot.gir(cluster_fit, cluster = cluster_dat$id,
                          cluster_order = "original", R = 3L,
                          seed = 17L, inference = FALSE)
cluster_boot$cluster$index  # 1 2 3 1 2 3
\end{verbatim}
An equivalent call supplies \code{cluster_dat$id[cluster_fit$row.index]} with
\code{cluster_order = "estimation"}. A retained-column formula \code{cluster = ~ id}
also gives the same grouping without a vector-order choice. Earlier analyses
that supplied misaligned vectors should rerun their bootstrap: inference can
change even when the cluster count is unchanged. Point estimates are unaffected.

\paragraph{\code{bootstrap_weights}.}
Optional positive finite multipliers, supplied as an
\(n_{\mathrm{used}}\times B\) matrix, with observations in rows and replicates
in columns. A numeric vector gives one replicate. The order must be the final
estimation-row order, not the original unfiltered data order. These are
multipliers, not original observation weights and not products of the two.
The code multiplies them by \code{fit$weights} once.

This argument allows paired comparisons: the same multiplier vector can be
passed to Legendre, spline, and oracle fits. Supplying
it consumes no random numbers in the internal GIR implementation. An external
\code{midcdf_boot} callback must also avoid extra randomness for this property
to apply to the entire computation.

\paragraph{\code{normalize_weights}.}
With \code{TRUE}, divide each supplied multiplier column by its own mean.
With \code{FALSE}, use and store the supplied entries unchanged. For the internal
estimator, including relative ridge handling, a common rescaling preserves
the draws up to floating-point error within usable numerical ranges. External
first-stage callbacks must respect weight scaling themselves. Generated exponential
multipliers are always normalized as in Algorithm 2, irrespective of this
option. Normalization is over the full estimation sample, not separately
inside each discrete cell.

Zero or negative supplied multipliers are rejected because they are not the
positive exponential-weight scheme implemented here. Original case weights
may be zero, as in \code{gir()}; their effective bootstrap weights remain zero.
Underflow or overflow in the combined weights is an error, not a reason to draw
a replacement multiplier vector.

\subsection{Arguments: first-stage refitting}

\paragraph{\code{first_stage_boot}.}
The default \code{"refit"} re-estimates the first stage for every multiplier
draw. It uses the original sample, cells, support transformation, knot locations,
constructed marginal dimensions, and tensor-product specification. The original
numerical singularity policy and clipping rule are applied again under the
bootstrap weights. The numerical rank and any dropped columns can therefore
change with the weights if the selected policy allows it; those changes are
recorded in the diagnostics.

The explicit alternative \code{"fixed"} holds the original mid-CDF vector
fixed and reweights only the second stage. It is suitable for an oracle first
stage, where the true conditional distribution is treated as known, or another
truly known nuisance. It is not the default for an estimated first stage and
produces a warning when inferential summaries are requested. Holding an
estimated first-stage vector fixed generally omits its estimation effect.

\paragraph{\code{midcdf_boot}.}
An optional callback for a point fit with an externally supplied first stage:
\begin{lstlisting}
function(object, weights, replicate) {
  # Re-estimate the external method under these combined weights.
  # Return one mid-CDF value per retained estimation row.
}
\end{lstlisting}
The \code{weights} argument contains the product of the original observation
weights and that replicate's multiplier vector, which is left unnormalized
when supplied with \code{normalize_weights = FALSE}. The callback must
return a finite numeric vector of length \code{nobs(object)} in the same row
order. The original clipping rule is subsequently applied.

A stored external mid-CDF vector alone is not enough to reconstruct how it
was estimated. Thus \code{first_stage_boot="refit"} errors for such a fit
unless this callback is supplied. A callback is not accepted for an internally
estimated series first stage or together with \code{first_stage_boot="fixed"}.
The callback's own statistical validity remains the researcher's responsibility.

\subsection{Arguments: output, failure handling and extra arguments}

\paragraph{\code{inference}.}
\code{TRUE} computes intervals, standard errors, bias summaries, and tests.
\code{FALSE} returns raw draws and diagnostics without those summaries. With
at least two successful stored draws, \code{summary(bb)} can compute summaries
later, even if the original call was draw-only. One draw alone is insufficient.

\paragraph{\code{keep_weights}.}
Retain the multiplier matrix as used in \code{bb$bootstrap_weights}; supplied
values remain unnormalized when \code{normalize_weights = FALSE}.
The default \code{FALSE} avoids storing an additional \(n\times B\) matrix.
These saved values are the multipliers, not the combined case weights.

\paragraph{\code{keep_midcdf}.}
Retain the first-stage fitted values for every draw in \code{bb$midcdf}, an
\(n\times B\) matrix. This is useful for nuisance diagnostics and
reproducibility checks,
but can use substantial memory. For example, \(n=100000\) and \(B=999\)
require roughly 800 MB for the numeric entries of one such matrix, before
object overhead. The default is \code{FALSE}.

\paragraph{\code{failure}.}
\code{"error"} stops at the first failed replicate with its number and reason.
This is the default. \code{"omit"} keeps the failed replicate's entire
coefficient row as \code{NA}, records the error, and computes summaries from
successful rows only, with a warning. It never redraws until a successful fit
appears. Omitting failures conditions on numerical success and can distort
inference; a substantial failure rate should be investigated, not accepted as
routine. An undefined coefficient transformation is an error even with this
option, because selectively removing transformed draws would change the
statistic's bootstrap distribution.

\paragraph{\code{...}.}
Only the formula convenience form passes these fully named options to
\code{gir()}. With a fitted object, any extra argument is an error. Thus a
misspelling or an attempted change of basis cannot silently leave the original
point estimator and bootstrap estimator different.

\subsection{The two-stage bootstrap calculation}

For clarity, write \(a_i\) for the original case weights (equal to one in the
paper's baseline sampling setting), and \(w_i^{*(b)}\) for the bootstrap
multipliers. For each replicate \(b\), draw
\[
e_i^{(b)}\stackrel{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim}\mathrm{Exp}(1),
\qquad \bar e^{(b)}=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n e_i^{(b)},
\qquad w_i^{*(b)}=e_i^{(b)}/\bar e^{(b)}.
\]
Let \(a_i^{*(b)}=a_iw_i^{*(b)}\). Within a discrete cell \(g\), form
\begin{align*}
Q_g^{*(b)}&=\sum_{j\in\mathcal I_g}a_j^{*(b)}p_jp_j',\\
S_g^{*(b)}(Y_i)&=\sum_{j\in\mathcal I_g}a_j^{*(b)}p_j
 \left\{\ind(Y_j<Y_i)+\tfrac12\ind(Y_j=Y_i)\right\},\\
\widehat M_i^{*(b)}&=p_i'\{Q_g^{*(b)}\}^{-1}S_g^{*(b)}(Y_i).
\end{align*}
The displayed inverse describes the unregularized full-rank calculation; the
explicitly selected singularity policy applies when necessary. With the same
weights, compute
\begin{align*}
A^{*(b)}&=\frac1n\sum_i a_i^{*(b)}Y_iX_iX_i',\\
b^{*(b)}&=\frac1n\sum_i a_i^{*(b)}Y_iX_i
                   \{2\widehat M_i^{*(b)}-1\},\\
\widehat\theta^{*(b)}&=\{A^{*(b)}\}^{-1}b^{*(b)}.
\end{align*}
Using different independent multiplier vectors in the two stages would discard
their joint resampling dependence and would not implement Algorithm 2.

Basis construction, sorting, and exact tie positions are cached once per
\code{bboot.gir()} call. Gram matrices and cumulative weighted scores must still
be recomputed for each draw. This retains the threshold-sorting
advantage without reconstructing every formula and basis \(B\) times. It does
not approximate thresholds or replace refitting by a fixed-first-stage shortcut.

The second-stage moments depend on \(X_i\) only through its value. Both
\code{gir()} and every bootstrap draw therefore first sum
\(a_i^{*(b)}Y_i\) and \(a_i^{*(b)}Y_i\{2\widehat M_i^{*(b)}-1\}\) over the
observations that share a regressor row, and then form \(A^{*(b)}\) and
\(b^{*(b)}\) from the distinct rows. The result is identical to the
observation-level formulas up to the order of floating-point summation. With
cell-level regressors the cost of a draw's second stage is proportional to the
number of distinct rows rather than to the sample size.

With \code{cluster}, the exponential values are drawn per cluster,
\(e_c^{(b)}\stackrel{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim}\mathrm{Exp}(1)\) for
\(c=1,\ldots,C\), and every observation \(i\) in cluster \(c(i)\) receives
\(w_i^{*(b)}=e_{c(i)}^{(b)}/\bar e^{(b)}\), with \(\bar e^{(b)}\) the mean over
the \(n\) observations. The two stages are computed exactly as above with
these multipliers.

Multiplying nonuniform case weights by iid multipliers is implemented as a
weighted extension. The paper's iid, unweighted validity theorem does
not alone justify design-based inference for complex surveys, clustered
multipliers, panel dependence, or time series. The function warns for
nonuniform/zero original weights when inference is requested. It supplies the
cluster option described above but no survey-design bootstrap.

\subsection{Basic confidence intervals: the paper's Algorithm 2}
\label{sec:basic}

Write \(B\) for the number of successful draws, \(\alpha=1-\code{level}\), and
\[
\Delta_{bj}=\widehat\theta_j^{*(b)}-\widehat\theta_j.
\]
For each coordinate, let \(q_{u,j}^*\) be the empirical quantile of these centered
\emph{unscaled} draws, computed by \code{stats::quantile(type=7)}. The interval is
\[
\boxed{\left[\widehat\theta_j-q_{1-\alpha/2,j}^*,\;
                   \widehat\theta_j-q_{\alpha/2,j}^*\right].}
\]
This is exactly the centered/basic construction in Algorithm 2. It is not
\([q_{\alpha/2}(\widehat\theta_j^*),q_{1-\alpha/2}(\widehat\theta_j^*)]\),
which would be a percentile interval. Despite the Bayesian-bootstrap weight
scheme, these reported intervals are frequentist basic confidence intervals,
not equal-tail posterior credible intervals.

The factor \(\sqrt n\) from the asymptotic theorem is unnecessary here. Were
one to take quantiles of \(\sqrt n\Delta_{bj}\), one would divide those quantiles
by \(\sqrt n\) when constructing the interval, obtaining the same endpoints.
Type-7 quantiles are R's default. A different quantile interpolation
rule can cause a finite-\(B\) discrepancy even with exactly the same draws.

\paragraph{Numerical example.}
Suppose \(\widehat\theta_j=0.20\), \(B=5\), and the centered draws are
\[
(-0.06,-0.03,-0.01,0.02,0.05).
\]
For an illustrative 80\% interval, type-7 interpolation gives
\(q_{0.1}^*=-0.048\) and \(q_{0.9}^*=0.038\). Thus the basic interval is
\[
[0.20-0.038,\;0.20-(-0.048)]=[0.162,0.248].
\]
The corresponding raw draws are \((0.14,0.17,0.19,0.22,0.25)\); their
percentile interval would instead be \([0.152,0.238]\). The methods differ.
Five replicates are used only to expose the arithmetic.

\subsection{Standard errors, covariance and bootstrap bias}
\label{sec:se}

Let
\(\bar\theta^*=B^{-1}\sum_{b=1}^B\widehat\theta^{*(b)}\). The implemented
coefficient covariance estimate is
\[
\widehat V_{\mathrm{boot}}=
 \frac1{B-1}\sum_{b=1}^B
 (\widehat\theta^{*(b)}-\bar\theta^*)
 (\widehat\theta^{*(b)}-\bar\theta^*)',
\qquad
\widehat{\mathrm{se}}_j=\sqrt{(\widehat V_{\mathrm{boot}})_{jj}}.
\]
These are \code{cov(bb$t[bb$success, ])} and the corresponding column standard
deviations. The covariance concerns \(\widehat\theta\), approximately
\(\Sigma/n\), not the limiting covariance \(\Sigma\) of
\(\sqrt n(\widehat\theta-\theta_0)\). No additional division by \(n\),
\(\sqrt n\), or \(\sqrt B\) is made.

Dividing the bootstrap standard deviation by \(\sqrt B\) estimates the
Monte Carlo error of a bootstrap \emph{average}, a different quantity. Nor is
an OLS residual degrees-of-freedom factor \(n-p\) used. The first-stage
estimation effect is captured by jointly refitting the two stages.

The reported bootstrap bias estimate is
\[
\widehat{\mathrm{bias}}_{\mathrm{boot}}=\bar\theta^*-\widehat\theta.
\]
It is descriptive and does not replace the point estimate by a bias-corrected
one. In the five-draw example, \(\bar\theta^*=0.194\), the bias estimate is
\(-0.006\), and the sample standard deviation is approximately \(0.0427785\).

\paragraph{What the theorem does and does not establish.}
Conditional weak convergence of the centered bootstrap law supports the stated
quantile construction at continuity points of the limiting distribution. By
itself it does \emph{not} imply convergence of its second moments. Interpreting
the conventional bootstrap covariance as a consistent variance estimator also
requires appropriate moment convergence, for example conditional uniform
integrability of squared scaled errors. The paper's proof does not separately
establish that additional result. This distinction, studied explicitly for
weighted semiparametric bootstraps by Cheng \cite{cheng}, also applies to
covariance-based prediction SEs and the common-covariance joint test. The code
computes the usual empirical covariance; it is not a substitute for checking
those additional regularity conditions.

\subsection{Smooth transformations and the bootstrap delta argument}

For \(h:\R^p\to\R^q\), set
\[
\widehat\eta=h(\widehat\theta),\qquad
\widehat\eta^{*(b)}=h(\widehat\theta^{*(b)}),\qquad
D_b=\widehat\eta^{*(b)}-\widehat\eta.
\]
Compute transformed basic intervals and standard deviations directly from these
transformed draws, not by transforming the already computed raw coefficient
interval endpoints. The latter operation is generally not equivalent for
nonlinear transformations or contrasts of correlated coefficients.

If \(h\) is continuously differentiable near \(\theta_0\) and its relevant
limiting derivative is nondegenerate, the first-order expansions are
\begin{align*}
\sqrt n\{h(\widehat\theta)-h(\theta_0)\}
  &=Dh(\theta_0)\sqrt n(\widehat\theta-\theta_0)+o_p(1),\\
\sqrt n\{h(\widehat\theta^*)-h(\widehat\theta)\}
  &=Dh(\theta_0)\sqrt n(\widehat\theta^*-\widehat\theta)
       +o_{P_w}(1).
\end{align*}
Thus, under the bootstrap theorem and the additional transformation regularity,
the second law approximates the first. This is the justification for the smooth
extensions, not a claim that every function accepted by R is regular.

For a fixed contrast \(L\theta\), the covariance is
\(L\widehat V_{\mathrm{boot}}L'\). In particular,
\[
\widehat{\mathrm{se}}(\widehat\theta_2-\widehat\theta_3)^2
 =\widehat V_{22}+\widehat V_{33}-2\widehat V_{23}.
\]
The off-diagonal covariance cannot generally be discarded.

\subsection{Marginal bootstrap hypothesis tests}
\label{sec:tests}

For \(H_0:\eta_{0j}=r_j\), let \(d_j=\widehat\eta_j-r_j\). The reference
draws are the \emph{centered errors} \(D_{bj}\), not the uncentered
\(\widehat\eta_j^{*(b)}\). The implemented tail probabilities are
\begin{align*}
\widehat p_{j,\mathrm{less}}
 &=\frac{1+\sum_{b=1}^B\ind\{D_{bj}\leq d_j\}}{B+1},\\
\widehat p_{j,\mathrm{greater}}
 &=\frac{1+\sum_{b=1}^B\ind\{D_{bj}\geq d_j\}}{B+1},\\
\widehat p_{j,\mathrm{two.sided}}
 &=\min\{1,\;2\min(\widehat p_{j,\mathrm{less}},
                            \widehat p_{j,\mathrm{greater}})\}.
\end{align*}
Under the null, both the observed deviation and the centered bootstrap error
have the same first-order limiting law, subject to the conditions just stated.
The equal-tail convention is chosen to match the tail logic of Algorithm 2.

The plus-one numerator/denominator is an explicit finite-simulation safeguard
against reporting a zero tail probability merely because no finite set of
draws exceeded the observed value. It vanishes as \(B\) increases. It does not
make this approximate bootstrap test an exact finite-sample randomization test.
The smallest possible reported two-sided p-value is \(2/(B+1)\), up to the
cap at one. Quantile interpolation and the plus-one rule mean that a finite-\(B\)
p-value decision and a type-7 interval exclusion can differ near a threshold;
no exact finite-\(B\) inversion equivalence is asserted.

If all transformed draws are identical, the numerical SE is zero but the
marginal p-value is set to \code{NA} with a warning. A degenerate computed
interval is not evidence that the parameter is known without uncertainty.

\subsection{Joint bootstrap Wald test}

Let \(\widehat V_h\) be the sample covariance of the transformed draws.
For \(H_0:h(\theta_0)=r\), the common-covariance statistics are
\[
W_{\mathrm{obs}}=(\widehat\eta-r)'\widehat V_h^{-1}(\widehat\eta-r),
\qquad W_b^*=D_b'\widehat V_h^{-1}D_b.
\]
The p-value is
\[
\widehat p_{\mathrm{joint}}
=\frac{1+\sum_{b=1}^B\ind\{W_b^*\geq W_{\mathrm{obs}}\}}{B+1}.
\]
The same covariance matrix is used in all these statistics. There is no
nested bootstrap and no replicate-specific standard-error estimate. A numerical
correlation-matrix scaling improves the rank check's invariance to measurement
units without changing this quadratic form.

Regular joint inference requires a full-row-rank restriction derivative at
the null and a positive-definite limiting restriction covariance, in addition
to the relevant bootstrap moment consistency. More draws cannot repair
mathematically redundant restrictions. The component named \code{df} records
the number of restrictions; the p-value above is bootstrap calibrated, not a
chi-squared or F tail computed from that number.

\subsection{Returned object and extraction methods}

The result has class \code{"gir_bootstrap"}. Its components \code{t0},
\code{t}, and \code{R} follow the naming convention of \code{boot}, but use
the GIR extraction methods listed below to work with this object.

\begin{longtable}{>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.30\textwidth}p{0.64\textwidth}}
\toprule
Component & Meaning \\
\midrule
\endhead
\code{fit} & The point-estimation object used, including its resolved data and basis specification.\\
\code{t0} & Named raw coefficient vector \(\widehat\theta\).\\
\code{t} & \(R\times p\) raw coefficient draws; failed rows are entirely \code{NA}.\\
\code{R}, \code{R.success}, \code{success} & Requested count, successful count, and logical success indicator.\\
\code{std.error}, \code{covariance} & Raw coefficient bootstrap SEs and covariance.\\
\code{conf.int}, \code{bias} & Raw basic intervals and bootstrap mean-minus-estimate bias.\\
\code{coefficient.table} & Raw Estimate, Boot. SE, Boot. bias, lwr and upr table.\\
\code{test} & Transformed estimate/null/deviation, SEs, covariance, basic intervals, marginal p-values, and optional joint test.\\
\code{level}, \code{quantile.type} & Requested level and quantile type 7.\\
\code{transform}, \code{null}, \code{alternative}, \code{joint} & Inferential specification.\\
\code{first_stage_boot} & Refitted, fixed, or external-refit mode.\\
\code{cluster} & \code{NULL}, or the number of clusters, the cluster index over estimation rows, and whether the first-stage cells are nested in the clusters.\\
\code{seed}, \code{rng.kind}, \code{rng.start} & Seed option, RNG kind, and captured starting RNG state.\\
\code{bootstrap_weights} & Optional saved multipliers as used, \(n\times R\).\\
\code{midcdf} & Optional saved first-stage draw matrix, \(n\times R\).\\
\code{diagnostics} & One row per attempted replicate, including success/error, conditioning and warning information.\\
\bottomrule
\end{longtable}

With \code{inference=FALSE}, the summary fields are \code{NULL}; draw matrices
and diagnostics remain available. Extraction and reanalysis use:
\begin{lstlisting}
coef(bb)                         # raw point coefficients, not bootstrap means
vcov(bb)                         # raw coefficient covariance
confint(bb, level = 0.90)         # raw coefficient basic intervals
confint(bb, parm = c("x2", "x3"))
summary(bb, transform = c(x2 = 1, x3 = -1), null = 0)
bb$test$conf.int                 # transformed intervals from the original call
bb$test$p.value
bb$test$joint
bb$diagnostics
\end{lstlisting}
\code{summary(bb)} can change the confidence level, transformation, null,
alternative, and joint-test choice without refitting or generating draws.
Its coefficient table concerns the raw coefficients; its \code{test} component
concerns the selected transformations. The original \code{bb} is not mutated
by obtaining a new summary.

The bootstrap diagnostics include the minimum first-stage reciprocal condition
number across cells, labels of cells receiving a numerical adjustment, the
number of out-of-range mid-CDF values before clipping, second-stage reciprocal
condition number, error messages, and a list of captured warning messages.
Original cellwise marginal dimension/knot vectors remain at
\code{bb$fit$first_stage$diagnostics}. The constructed basis allocation is fixed
throughout that bootstrap run.

\section{\texorpdfstring{\code{predict.gir}: linear conditional Gini predictions}{predict.gir: linear conditional Gini predictions}}
\label{sec:prediction}

\subsection{Name, target and usage}

\code{predict.gir()} evaluates the fitted linear conditional Gini index at
specified covariates. It is normally invoked through the standard generic
\code{predict(fit, ...)}. The target is
\[
G(x)=x'\theta_0\quad\hbox{under correct specification},
\qquad \widehat G(x)=x'\widehat\theta.
\]
Under misspecification, \(x'\theta_0\) is the fitted linear-approximation target
identified in the paper, not necessarily the true CGIF at each individual \(x\).
Confidence statements concern that approximation target when the model is
misspecified.

The method does not predict \(Y\), \(E[Y\mid X]\), or \(M(y\mid x)\).
A model for the conditional Gini alone does not specify the conditional law
needed for individual-outcome prediction intervals.

\begin{lstlisting}
predict.gir(
  object,
  newdata = NULL,
  se.fit = FALSE,
  interval = c("none", "confidence"),
  level = 0.95,
  bootstrap = NULL,
  R = 999L,
  seed = NULL,
  na.action = stats::na.pass,
  ...
)
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Arguments}

\paragraph{\code{object}.}
A current \code{gir} fit. New uncertainty calculations require an identified,
full-rank coefficient specification. Point predictions from aliased fits are
allowed only where the requested linear function is estimable; see below.

\paragraph{\code{newdata}.}
A data frame containing the raw predictor variables used by the second-stage
formula. The response and variables used only in the first stage are not needed.
\code{NULL} uses the original retained second-stage design and produces values
in the same order as \code{fitted(fit)}. That order contains the estimation
sample only; it does not reinsert observations removed by the original fit,
even when the original fitting action was \code{na.exclude}. Use
\code{fit$row.index} to map to original data rows.

For new data, the method uses the original model terms, contrasts, and factor
levels. Training parameters stored by safe formula transformations such as
\code{poly(x, 2)} or \code{splines::bs(x, ...)} are reused. New factor levels
are errors; missing required predictors are errors rather than an intentional
fallback to an unrelated vector in the calling environment. Custom formula
functions must themselves support meaningful evaluation on new observations.

\paragraph{\code{se.fit}.}
If \code{FALSE}, return a prediction vector or interval matrix. If
\code{TRUE}, return a list with components \code{fit}, \code{se.fit},
\code{level}, \code{method}, and \code{R}. SEs concern the estimated linear
index, not observation noise. Setting this option requests a bootstrap when
none is supplied.

\paragraph{\code{interval}.}
\code{"none"} returns point values. \code{"confidence"} returns a matrix
with columns \code{fit}, \code{lwr}, and \code{upr}, using pointwise basic
bootstrap intervals. There is no \code{"prediction"} option for individual
outcomes and no simultaneous confidence-band option in this version.

\paragraph{\code{level}.}
Confidence level strictly between zero and one. Its default is 0.95, even if
an existing bootstrap object was originally summarized at a different level.
The stored draws can be reused at the level requested here.

\paragraph{\code{bootstrap}.}
An optional matching result of \code{bboot.gir(fit, ...)}. Reusing it is the
preferred method when several predictions or transformed tests are needed.
Compatibility is checked against the raw coefficient vector, estimation
snapshot, first-stage specification, and point mid-CDF vector. A bootstrap of
a different sample or modified estimator is rejected. Raw coefficient draws
are used even if the supplied bootstrap result also contains tests of nonlinear
transformations.

\paragraph{\code{R} and \code{seed}.}
Used only for an automatic \code{bboot.gir()} call when uncertainty is requested
and \code{bootstrap=NULL}. They have the same meaning as in that function.
Do not supply these controls together with an existing bootstrap result.
Explicit bootstrap controls without \code{se.fit=TRUE} or
\code{interval="confidence"} are errors. A pure point-prediction call draws
no random numbers.

\paragraph{\code{na.action}.}
Applies to \code{newdata}, not to the already fitted sample. The default
\code{na.pass} keeps positions corresponding to missing predictors and reports
\code{NA} predictions/SEs there. \code{na.omit} removes incomplete rows.
\code{na.exclude} removes them for matrix construction and reinserts their
missing positions in the returned prediction and SE objects. \code{na.fail}
stops. Infinite transformed design values are rejected.
Empty \code{newdata}, or omission of all prediction rows, returns a
length-zero point vector or a zero-row confidence-interval matrix with columns
\code{fit}, \code{lwr} and \code{upr}. With \code{se.fit=TRUE}, the standard-error
vector has matching length. \code{na.exclude} restores \code{NA} positions,
including when all prediction rows were missing.

\paragraph{\code{...}.}
Extra \code{bboot.gir()} controls for an automatic bootstrap only, for example
\code{cluster} and \code{cluster_order}, or setting \code{first_stage_boot} to
\code{"fixed"} for a known nuisance. Cluster vectors describe the original fitting
data or estimation sample, never \code{newdata}. These controls are
not \code{gir()} options and cannot change the point fit. With an existing
bootstrap, extra controls are rejected. A simple prediction needs none of them.

\subsection{Prediction standard errors and intervals}

For a row vector \(x'\), define the bootstrap predictions
\[
\widehat G^{*(b)}(x)=x'\widehat\theta^{*(b)}.
\]
Their sample standard deviation is, up to floating-point arithmetic,
\[
\boxed{\widehat{\mathrm{se}}\{\widehat G(x)\}
       =\sqrt{x'\widehat V_{\mathrm{boot}}x}.}
\]
The confidence interval follows the same basic construction as Algorithm 2,
now for a fixed linear functional:
\[
\boxed{\left[\widehat G(x)-q_{1-\alpha/2}^*(x),\;
                   \widehat G(x)-q_{\alpha/2}^*(x)\right],}
\]
where \(q_u^*(x)\) is the type-7 quantile of
\(\{x'(\widehat\theta^{*(b)}-\widehat\theta):1\leq b\leq B\}\).
Coefficient estimation uncertainty from both stages is already represented in
these joint draws. No OLS residual variance is added.

For an intercept and one covariate,
\[
\widehat{\mathrm{se}}\{\widehat G(x)\}^2
  =\widehat V_{00}+2x\widehat V_{01}+x^2\widehat V_{11}.
\]
For example, suppose
\(\widehat\theta=(0.10,0.20)'\) and
\[
\widehat V=\begin{pmatrix}0.0004&-0.0001\\-0.0001&0.0009\end{pmatrix}.
\]
At \(x=0.5\), the fitted Gini is 0.20 and its variance estimate is
\(0.0004-0.0001+0.000225=0.000525\), giving an SE of approximately 0.022913.
The basic interval still requires the projected draws; this variance number
alone does not determine its possibly asymmetric endpoints.

Adding the marginal lower coefficient bounds to obtain a lower prediction
bound is generally incorrect. Predictions use the same joint coefficient draw
for all terms, preserving covariance. Computation is divided into blocks of
prediction rows to avoid storing the entire newdata-by-bootstrap matrix at once.

\subsection{Pointwise coverage, range and extrapolation}

Each interval applies to the specified fixed covariate row. A collection of
pointwise 95\% intervals is not a 95\% simultaneous band over all rows or over
a continuum of \(x\). No claim of uniform coverage is made for a growing or
post-selected prediction grid.

Neither predictions nor confidence endpoints are clipped to \([0,1]\).
Out-of-range point predictions cause a warning and remain visible. This is
separate from \code{gir(clip=TRUE)}, which clips first-stage mid-CDF values.
A constrained nonlinear Gini model or interval transformation would be a
separate specification, not a display change.

The linear index can be evaluated outside the observed covariate range, but
this is extrapolation. The method does not guarantee overlap for an unobserved
joint categorical profile or correctness of the linear model outside the
support used for estimation.

For an aliased second-stage fit, a new linear functional must lie in the
estimable row space of the weighted training design. Non-estimable rows return
\code{NA} with a warning. Bootstrap prediction inference nevertheless requires
a full-rank coefficient specification in this implementation. Refitting the
same identified model without redundant columns is preferable to obtaining
coefficient uncertainty for an arbitrary alias convention.

\section{Complete worked examples}
\label{sec:worked}

\subsection{Generate data from the paper's continuous-binary design}

The following is a self-contained setup using base R. It draws one dataset
from the paper's simulation design with one continuous and two binary
regressors.
\begin{lstlisting}
library(GIR)

set.seed(150L)
n <- 2000L
x1 <- runif(n, 0.1, 1)
x2 <- rbinom(n, 1L, 0.5)
x3 <- rbinom(n, 1L, 0.5)
m <- 0.075*x1 + 0.100*x2 + 0.125*x3
alpha <- (1 - m) / (2*m)
y <- rbeta(n, shape1 = alpha, shape2 = 1)
dat <- data.frame(y = y, x1 = x1, x2 = x2, x3 = x3)
\end{lstlisting}
The true coefficient vector is \((0.075,0.100,0.125)'\), with no second-stage
intercept. The true conditional CDF and mid-CDF coincide because the response
law is continuous: \(M(y\mid x)=y^{\alpha(x)}\) on \([0,1]\).

\subsection{Fit and bootstrap the spline specification}

\begin{lstlisting}
fit <- gir(
  y ~ 0 + x1 + x2 + x3, data = dat,
  first_stage = ~ x1 + x2 + x3,
  continuous = "x1", discrete = c("x2", "x3"),
  basis = "spline", spline_order = 4L,
  kappa = 0.300, basis_constant = 1,
  sieve_size_type = "knots", sieve_size_reference = "cell",
  basis_bounds = list(x1 = c(0.1, 1)), clip = FALSE,
  first_stage_singular = "ridge", first_stage_tol = 1e-12,
  ridge = 1e-10, singular.ok = FALSE
)

bb <- bboot.gir(fit, R = 999L, level = 0.95, seed = 42L)
summary(fit)                 # point-estimator summary
summary(bb)                  # bootstrap inference summary
coef(bb)                     # same point coefficients as coef(fit)
confint(bb)                  # Algorithm 2 intervals
bb$std.error
vcov(bb)
bb$bias
bb$diagnostics
fit$first_stage$diagnostics$marginal_dimensions
fit$first_stage$diagnostics$marginal_knot_counts
\end{lstlisting}
This call selects \code{sieve_size_type = "knots"} explicitly, so that
\(\lfloor n_g^{0.3}\rfloor\) is the number of interior knots; the package
default applies the rule to the total dimension instead.
With this seed, some series mid-CDF estimates fall outside \([0,1]\), so
the fit and bootstrap report warnings. Inspect \code{midcdf(fit)} and
\code{bb$diagnostics}. The example retains those estimates; choosing
\code{clip = TRUE} would change the estimator.

\subsection{The formula convenience form}

\begin{lstlisting}
bb_formula <- bboot.gir(
  y ~ 0 + x1 + x2 + x3,
  data = dat,
  first_stage = ~ x1 + x2 + x3,
  continuous = "x1", discrete = c("x2", "x3"),
  basis = "spline", spline_order = 4L,
  sieve_size_type = "knots", sieve_size_reference = "cell",
  basis_bounds = list(x1 = c(0.1, 1)),
  first_stage_singular = "ridge", singular.ok = FALSE,
  R = 999L, level = 0.95, seed = 42L
)
# Same model, defaults and local seed as the preceding example.
all.equal(bb$t, bb_formula$t, tolerance = 1e-12)
\end{lstlisting}
The fitted-object form is shorter and more resistant to accidentally changing
an option between estimation and inference.

\subsection{Contrasts, Gini points and a coefficient ratio}

\begin{lstlisting}
# Difference between two coefficients: H0 theta_x2 - theta_x3 = 0.
s_diff <- summary(bb, transform = c(x2 = 1, x3 = -1), null = 0)
s_diff$test$table
s_diff$test$conf.int

# Express the x2 coefficient in Gini points (100 times the index).
s_points <- summary(bb, transform = c(x2 = 100), null = 0)
s_points$test$table

# Nonlinear example; appropriate only for a well-separated nonzero denominator.
ratio <- function(b) c(ratio = unname(b["x2"] / b["x3"]))
s_ratio <- summary(bb, transform = ratio, null = 1)
s_ratio$test$table
\end{lstlisting}
These calls reuse the same raw draws. They do not require an analytic derivative
from the user, but smoothness and nondegeneracy are still assumptions. A ratio
interval can be unreliable near a zero denominator even when no numeric error
is raised.

\subsection{Joint and directional tests}

\begin{lstlisting}
# Nonredundant joint restrictions: H0 theta_x2 = theta_x3 = 0.
selected <- function(b) b[c("x2", "x3")]
s_joint <- summary(bb, transform = selected, null = c(x2 = 0, x3 = 0),
                    joint = TRUE)
s_joint$test$joint

# Directional marginal alternative: theta_x2 > 0.
s_greater <- summary(bb, transform = c(x2 = 1), null = 0,
                      alternative = "greater")
s_greater$test$p.value
\end{lstlisting}
The reported confidence intervals remain two-sided; only the test changes
with \code{alternative}. The joint test above tests the vector of two
restrictions and is not equivalent to requiring that both marginal p-values
fall below the same significance level.

\subsection{Newdata predictions with a reusable bootstrap}

\begin{lstlisting}
newdat <- data.frame(
  x1 = c(0.2, 0.5, 0.8),
  x2 = c(0, 1, 1),
  x3 = c(0, 0, 1)
)
predict(fit, newdata = newdat)
predict(fit)                         # identical to fitted(fit)

pred <- predict(fit, newdata = newdat,
                interval = "confidence", se.fit = TRUE,
                level = 0.95, bootstrap = bb)
pred$fit
pred$se.fit

# Reuse the same coefficient draws at another level.
predict(fit, newdata = newdat, interval = "confidence",
        level = 0.90, bootstrap = bb)
\end{lstlisting}
This gives conditional Gini predictions for the listed profiles, not predicted
wages or incomes. No outcome column is required in \code{newdat}.

\subsection{Automatic bootstrap inside prediction}

\begin{lstlisting}
predict(fit, newdata = newdat, se.fit = TRUE,
        interval = "confidence", R = 999L, seed = 42L)
\end{lstlisting}
This is convenient for one prediction request. For multiple requests, obtaining
\code{bb <- bboot.gir(fit, ...)} once and reusing it avoids repeated fitting and
makes comparisons share the same bootstrap randomness.

\subsection{A difference in predicted Gini indices}

For two fixed profiles \(x_A,x_B\), the difference is
\((x_A-x_B)'\theta\). In the no-intercept worked model:
\begin{lstlisting}
contrast_AB <- c(x1 = 0.8 - 0.2, x2 = 1 - 0, x3 = 1 - 0)
s_AB <- summary(bb, transform = contrast_AB, null = 0)
s_AB$test$table
\end{lstlisting}
Do not subtract the two separately computed prediction interval endpoints.
The contrast calculation retains their dependence through the common coefficient
draws. For a model with interactions, construct \(x_A-x_B\) from its full
second-stage model-matrix rows, not merely differences of raw data columns.

\subsection{Supplied multipliers and exact reuse across bases}

\begin{lstlisting}
# Draw a single vector once, after generating one simulated dataset.
e <- rexp(nobs(fit), rate = 1)
w_star <- e / mean(e)

one_spline <- bboot.gir(
  fit, R = 1L, inference = FALSE,
  bootstrap_weights = w_star, normalize_weights = FALSE
)
one_spline$t[1L, ]

fit_legendre <- gir(
  y ~ 0 + x1 + x2 + x3, data = dat,
  continuous = "x1", discrete = c("x2", "x3"),
  basis = "legendre", kappa = 0.245,
  sieve_size_type = "dimension", sieve_size_reference = "cell",
  basis_bounds = list(x1 = c(0.1, 1)),
  first_stage_singular = "ridge", first_stage_tol = 1e-12,
  ridge = 1e-10, singular.ok = FALSE
)
one_legendre <- bboot.gir(
  fit_legendre, R = 1L, inference = FALSE,
  bootstrap_weights = w_star, normalize_weights = FALSE
)
\end{lstlisting}
Calling each basis variant with independently generated weights would not
give paired bootstrap draws.

\subsection{The oracle first stage}

\begin{lstlisting}
dat$oracle_midcdf <- dat$y^alpha
fit_oracle <- gir(y ~ 0 + x1 + x2 + x3, data = dat,
                  midcdf = oracle_midcdf, singular.ok = FALSE)
one_oracle <- bboot.gir(
  fit_oracle, R = 1L, inference = FALSE,
  first_stage_boot = "fixed",
  bootstrap_weights = w_star, normalize_weights = FALSE
)
\end{lstlisting}
This is the oracle calculation of the paper's simulations: the true
conditional CDF is held fixed. It does not justify holding a data-estimated
mid-CDF fixed in an empirical analysis.

\subsection{A complete external first-stage callback example}

Here a single unconditional mid-distribution is intentionally supplied to an
intercept-only model. The callback re-estimates that same object under the
combined weights, using an explicit loop only for clarity:
\begin{lstlisting}
small <- data.frame(y = c(0, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 5))
weighted_mid <- function(y, a) {
  vapply(y, function(yi) {
    sum(a * ((y < yi) + 0.5*(y == yi))) / sum(a)
  }, numeric(1))
}
small$m <- weighted_mid(small$y, rep(1, nrow(small)))
external_fit <- gir(y ~ 1, data = small, midcdf = m)
refit_external <- function(object, weights, replicate) {
  weighted_mid(object$estimation$y, weights)
}
external_bb <- bboot.gir(external_fit, R = 999L, seed = 8L,
                         midcdf_boot = refit_external)
summary(external_bb)
\end{lstlisting}
The callback uses the actual retained sample, not a separately cleaned data
vector. A general external conditional estimator needs its own weighted
refitting implementation; the simple loop above is not a replacement for it.

\subsection{Missing prediction values}

\begin{lstlisting}
nd_na <- newdat
nd_na$x1[2L] <- NA_real_
predict(fit, nd_na)                         # middle entry is NA
predict(fit, nd_na, na.action = na.omit)    # two entries
predict(fit, nd_na, na.action = na.exclude) # three entries, middle NA
# predict(fit, nd_na, na.action = na.fail)  # intentional error
\end{lstlisting}
For uncertainty, the same row policy applies to the interval matrix and SEs.

\section{Relation to existing R interfaces}

\paragraph{\code{quantreg::boot.rq}.}
The documented function is a lower-level regression bootstrap engine taking
\code{x}, \code{y}, \code{tau}, and \code{R}, with a matrix of coefficient
draws returned for subsequent analysis. Its \code{bsmethod="wxy"} uses unit
exponential weights \cite{bootrq}. The GIR package retains the regression-specific
resampling logic, replicate count, and access to raw draws, but accepts a
fitted model to preserve the two-stage specification. It adds the paper's basic
intervals and an explicit smooth-transformation/test interface. It does not
call a quantile regression engine or import \code{quantreg} at runtime.

\paragraph{\code{boot::boot}.}
The generic \code{boot()} interface takes data and a statistic and can pass
extra arguments through \code{...}; its familiar \code{t0}/\code{t}/\code{R}
layout motivates the output of \code{bboot.gir()} \cite{bootmanual}. However,
\code{stype="w"} only tells \code{boot()} that a statistic's second argument
represents weights. It does not, by itself, request iid exponential Bayesian
multipliers. A generic wrapper is possible, but the dedicated engine makes the
required weight law, common two-stage weighting, fixed basis, tie handling, and
cached calculation explicit. The class returned by \code{bboot.gir()} is not a \code{boot}
object, and \code{boot.ci()} is not the supported extraction method.

\paragraph{\code{predict.rq} and \code{predict.lm}.}
The fitted-object/newdata design, \code{interval}, \code{level}, and
\code{na.action} choices follow familiar regression conventions; the
\code{se.fit} list form is especially close to \code{predict.lm}
\cite{predictrq,predictlm}. The statistical target and uncertainty calculation
differ. \code{predict.rq} has several interval approaches, including percentile
bootstrap prediction-function intervals, whereas this version of the GIR package implements
only Algorithm 2's basic logic. Unlike \code{predict.lm}, \code{predict.gir()} does not add an
individual-outcome noise term or provide a residual scale and Student degrees
of freedom. There is only one prediction scale here: the linear Gini index.

\section{Using the package in a Monte Carlo study}
\label{sec:validation}

\subsection{Three different quantities called standard deviation or bias}

For a real dataset, \code{bb$std.error} estimates sampling uncertainty from
many bootstrap draws conditional on that dataset. Its \code{bias} is the
bootstrap mean minus the fitted coefficient.

Across Monte Carlo datasets, a simulation study instead computes
\[
\widehat{\mathrm{Bias}}_{\mathrm{MC}}
 =\frac1S\sum_{r=1}^S\widehat\theta^{(r)}-\theta_0,
\quad
\widehat{\mathrm{SD}}_{\mathrm{MC},j}
 =\left\{\frac1{S-1}\sum_{r=1}^S
 (\widehat\theta_j^{(r)}-\bar\theta_j)^2\right\}^{1/2}.
\]
These are not \code{bb$bias} or a within-dataset bootstrap SE; each quantity
should be compared with its own counterpart.

\subsection{Warp-speed coverage}

The warp-speed method \cite{gpw} uses one bootstrap draw per Monte Carlo
dataset and pools
\[
\Delta_{rj}=\widehat\theta_j^{*(r)}-\widehat\theta_j^{(r)}
\quad(r=1,\ldots,S).
\]
Let \(q_j^-\) and \(q_j^+\) be the pooled type-7 quantiles at
\(\alpha/2\) and \(1-\alpha/2\). The dataset-specific intervals are
\[
[\widehat\theta_j^{(r)}-q_j^+,\;
 \widehat\theta_j^{(r)}-q_j^-],
\]
and coverage is the fraction containing the known truth. This pooling is
distinct from the many-draw conditional bootstrap of Algorithm 2.

In such a study, obtain each dataset's point estimate with \code{gir()} and
its single draw with \code{bboot.gir()} using \code{R=1} and
\code{inference=FALSE}. When several estimators are compared on the same
dataset, supply the same multiplier vector to each of them through
\code{bootstrap_weights}, so that their draws are paired. Running \(B=999\)
draws inside every Monte Carlo dataset is a different experiment.

For already assembled \(S\times p\) matrices \code{theta_hat} and
\code{theta_star}, the aggregation is:
\begin{lstlisting}
alpha_level <- 1 - confidence_level
delta <- theta_star - theta_hat
q_lo <- apply(delta, 2L, quantile, probs = alpha_level/2,
              names = FALSE, type = 7L)
q_hi <- apply(delta, 2L, quantile, probs = 1-alpha_level/2,
              names = FALSE, type = 7L)
ci_lo <- sweep(theta_hat, 2L, q_hi, "-")
ci_hi <- sweep(theta_hat, 2L, q_lo, "-")
truth <- matrix(rep(true_theta, each = nrow(theta_hat)),
                nrow = nrow(theta_hat))
coverage <- colMeans(ci_lo <= truth & truth <= ci_hi)
mc_bias <- colMeans(theta_hat) - true_theta
mc_sd <- apply(theta_hat, 2L, sd)
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Mathematical validation}

The source tests include independent finite-sample checks tied to the paper:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
\item \code{test-math-algorithms.R} implements Algorithms 1 and 2 directly
      with QR-based threshold regressions. It checks full-rank Legendre and
      spline tensor bases, unequal discrete cells, ties and zeros, and paired
      bootstrap refits using the same multipliers in both stages.
\item \code{test-math-gini-identity.R} checks Numerical Equivalence using
      pairwise absolute differences. It verifies the empirical group Gini,
      without the optional finite-sample correction, and its weighted
      counterpart in saturated bootstrap draws.
\item \code{test-math-invariance.R} checks Scale Invariance for positive
      outcome rescaling, and row-order invariance when multiplier rows move
      with the observations. Coefficients, predictions, goodness of fit and
      paired bootstrap inference are compared.
\item \code{test-math-r-squared.R} checks Algorithm 3, the weighted variation
      decomposition, and nested second-stage comparisons with the first stage
      held fixed. Zero benchmark variation is undefined; a model without an
      intercept can have negative GIR-$R^2$.
\end{itemize}
These fixtures use no ridge or clipping and run without network access or the
draft paper. The inference arithmetic checks use 40 deterministic multiplier
columns and confidence level 0.5; this is a small software test, not a coverage
experiment or an applied-inference recommendation. General comparisons use
tolerance \(10^{-10}\), and small pairwise identities use \(10^{-12}\).
The reference calculations do not call GIR's internal basis or fitting helpers.
The separate cluster and ridge tests verify implementation extensions without
establishing their theoretical validity.

From the source directory, run \code{testthat::test_local(filter="math-")}
for these checks, or \code{testthat::test_local()} for the full suite.
They also run under \code{R CMD check}. The installed help topic
\code{help("GIR-validation", package="GIR")} provides the detailed mapping
and qualifications. Passing the tests does not prove asymptotic results or
nominal confidence-interval coverage; the simulation framework above is a
separate validation exercise.

Additional checks in \code{test-empty-samples.R} cover empty samples, retained
weights, custom missing-value actions, alignment and omission metadata, and
empty prediction/interval shapes. The point-estimator and bootstrap tests check the complete set of expected
warnings and require other calls to run without warnings. Run \code{testthat::test_local(filter="empty-samples")} to isolate
the sample-handling tests.

\section{Installation and development}

Before the first CRAN release, installation from GitHub requires access to
the private repository:
\begin{lstlisting}
# install.packages("remotes")
remotes::install_github("uptoaparameter/GIR", build_vignettes = TRUE)
library(GIR)
?gir
?bboot.gir
?predict.gir
\end{lstlisting}
The help files in \code{man/} are generated from the roxygen comments in
\code{R/}; after editing those comments, run
\code{roxygen2::roxygenize()} in the package directory.
Run \code{testthat::test_local()} for the tests. Full release checks build
the source package and run \code{R CMD check --as-cran}, including this
vignette and the function reference. Maintainer instructions are in
\code{tools/README.md} in the repository.

Open the installed guide with \code{vignette("GIR-guide", package = "GIR")}
and obtain the package and paper citations with \code{citation("GIR")}.

The fit and bootstrap objects retain resolved estimation data, even when
\code{model=FALSE}, \code{x=FALSE}, and \code{y=FALSE} omit corresponding public
slots. Those flags do not anonymize an object or remove its internal snapshot.
Do not publish fitted objects containing confidential data without a separate
review of their contents.


\section{Current limitations}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
\item Bootstrap inference uses iid or cluster-level exponential multipliers;
      no complex-survey or time-series resampling scheme is implemented, and
      the paper's theorem covers the iid case.
\item Prediction intervals are pointwise confidence intervals for the linear
      Gini index, not individual-outcome intervals or simultaneous bands.
\item Bootstrap coefficient and prediction inference require a full-rank
      second-stage specification. Nonregular transformations require separate
      methods; a numerical result alone does not establish validity.
\item The paper's weak-convergence proof is not itself a proof of bootstrap
      variance moment consistency. Covariance-based inference requires the
      additional regularity explained above.
\item Numerical clipping, regularization, and external first-stage methods do
      not automatically inherit the unmodified series estimator's theory.
\item Full tensor products can become large when the number of continuous
      regressors increases.
\item Discrete conditioning is fully saturated through joint cells, which can
      create sparse groups when many discrete variables or levels are used.
\item Interior spline knots are currently equally spaced on the normalized
      support; custom or quantile-spaced knot locations are not yet exposed.
\item The first-stage fitted function is not constrained to be monotone in the
      outcome threshold or to lie in \([0,1]\), except for optional pointwise
      clipping at the observed evaluation pairs.
\end{itemize}

